window.harnessChunks["rows-f26483d832defa90.js"]=["<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-23T22:28:54+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ccxxxyy/mini-code-agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Mini-Code-Agent</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-23\">2026-10-23</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ccxxxyy/mini-code-agent/tree/ee8c4339b0dd04088377f0c27e04abc1bf9904b9\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision ee8c4339b0dd04088377f0c27e04abc1bf9904b9\">ee8c433 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: execute developer operations using model-decided reading, patching, shell, search, task and optionally delegated child-agent actions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing code/user goals, test and tool failures, permission denials, child/peer reports and varying repository state. - Decisive decision or feedback right: each AgentLoop selects and revises substantive tool operations from observed results; child models own their bounded local operational decisions. - Decision owner: configured primary model and separately instantiated child models. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tool registry, async streaming, sandbox/permission manager, context compression, memory retrieval, event bus, tool result cache and differentiated child policies. - Closure path: task/context → model-selected tool calls → actual tool result/denial → feedback event/conversation → next model decision or completion. - Boundary reachability: Application constructs the primary loop and tool registry; SubAgentManager instantiates independent AgentLoop children for /spawn, model spawnagents and /team in shipped modes. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model-backed loops select task-specific action and repair, unlike fixed enforcement and scheduling code. - Evidence: AgentLoop, SubAgent, application. - Basis: frozen executable source and mock-driven script tests. - Confidence: high for actual agent operating construction; not a live capability benchmark. - Caveats: provider calls and real model outcomes were not executed; one native subagent test hung under the local runner.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: regulate interference between concurrently deciding read/write child agents and dependent coding steps by model-selected isolated workspace and planned dependency boundaries. - Disturbance / variety regulated: parallel worker file collisions, premature execution before dependent research is complete and competing modifications of a shared repository tree. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the primary model selects the isolated option and delegated work topology through spawnagents; model-backed Planner selects per-step dependson/writesfiles, while the runtime enforces separate worktrees/read-only tool restrictions and dependency batches. - Decision owner: primary and planning models for coordination choices, deterministic SubAgentManager, AgentTeam and WorktreeManager for execution/enforcement. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: per-child Git worktrees, read-only child tool filtering, independent child task scheduling, dependency result gates and returned child/merge reports. - Closure path: potential overlap/dependency → model chooses isolation/ordered plan → independent S1s execute in bounded trees/batches → output/failure and dependency reports return to parent → subsequent task assignment can change. - Boundary reachability: shipped model tool spawnagents exposes isolated=true, which passes isolation=&#39;worktree&#39; to SubAgentManager; /team creates model-backed Planner plus team batches and passes dependency results to later workers. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: selecting isolation and cross-task dependencies is discretionary model judgment, while worktree creation, tool filtering and dependency checks enforce the selected relation. This is optional and not an unconditional guarantee of global conflict-free merging. - Evidence: spawn tool, team, planner, worktree. - Basis: direct frozen coordination paths and isolated mock/team/worktree tests. - Confidence: medium-high for the bounded model-selected coordination choice and enforcement. - Caveats: defaults permit non-isolated children; dependency planning can make poor choices and Git branch integration remains a separate source of unresolved conflicts. - Distinct S1 units: independent model-backed SubAgent/pane workers running separate conversation/tool loops. - Inter-S1 disturbance: overlapping edits in one repository tree and tasks attempted before prerequisites complete. - Attenuating coordination relation: model-selected worktree isolation plus planned dependency batches and automatic removal of write tools from read-only team members. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: child results and prior-step outputs enter the next team prompt or primary spawnagents tool result; interference/error signals can change later work. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: code explicitly changes the file-write collision topology and prevents dependency-violating/write-unauthorized peer execution rather than merely forwarding messages.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: provide the legitimate local operator a whole-current roster of spawned agent commitments and targeted intervention control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: stuck, obsolete or competing-priority background children continuing to use finite time and tool/model resources. - Decisive decision or feedback right: an attending user invokes /spawn list, decides which active child should stop, and calls /spawn cancel &lt;id&gt; or cancel-all. - Decision owner: human operator as parent for the current session; fixed SubAgentManager.cancel implements the selected stop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: listactive, getstatus, activesnapshots, current-agent progress board and cancel hooks propagated to child AgentLoop. - Closure path: whole-current list/phase → human chooses specific running agent → /spawn cancel &lt;id&gt; → SubAgentManager.cancel aborts its loop/task → changed active roster/remaining commitments. - Boundary reachability: Application wires the manager into first-party slash commands; /spawn list and /spawn cancel &lt;id&gt; are direct regular installed command handlers, independent of optional external governance. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the human operator decides a whole-current intervention, while the manager enforces it. The model-facing spawnagents can create children and receive results, but has no equivalent first-party current full-roster/selective-cancel tool; hence P, not A(P). - Evidence: slash commands, manager roster/cancel, progress UI. - Basis: first-party human current roster/action path and deterministic command tests. - Confidence: medium-high for bounded session control. - Caveats: human supervision is optional and requires attention; resource budgeting outside active child roster and independent installations is not proven. - Whole-system current view: /spawn list enumerates every active child id and phase in the current session; the board displays current child snapshots and progress. - Current-control decision scope: operator-selected cancellation of one or all current child agent commitments, changing ongoing work rather than merely gating an isolated file edit.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: provide a separate read-only model-run verifier to inspect a stated condition and issue an independent structured PASS/FAIL verdict. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incorrect or incomplete claims from normal coder output that ordinary model self-reporting or passing task actions can overlook. - Decisive decision or feedback right: an optional verify child independently reads code/runs read-only checks, calls syntheticoutput with verdict/failures and returns its report; the parent/operator is free to act on it. - Decision owner: separately instantiated verification model for its judgment; user/parent for optional invocation and follow-through. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: distinct verify type prompt, restricted read-only tool list, isolated child conversation, syntheticoutput extraction and SubAgentResult.structuredoutput. - Closure path: user/parent selects verify child → separate source inspection and structured verdict → returned child output → possible parent/user correction. No mandatory or universally automatic correction gate is enforced. - Boundary reachability: agenttype=&#39;verify&#39; is a shipped spawnagents input and /spawn --type verify command option; SubAgent selects getagenttype policy/tools and extracts the structured result from its own transcript. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the auditor&#39;s model owns a distinct evidence-based verdict rather than merely formatting the coder&#39;s report, but independent audit→enforced current-control corrective closure needs explicit composition; therefore C rather than A. - Evidence: types/verify prompt, child tool/structured output, spawn tool. - Basis: directly exposed first-party verifier constructor, source-enforced tool restriction and offline type tests. - Confidence: medium for meaningful optional independent review construction, not reviewed model quality. - Caveats: reviewer is not necessarily institutionally/provider independent; a user-defined code-review skill or hash-chain audit log alone is not promoted to S3 and no mandatory audit-controlled repair was observed. - Claim being audited: condition or correctness claim about the working project and its implementation. - Ordinary reporting path: primary worker&#39;s own tool observations, edits/tests and completion summary. - Complementary access path: a fresh child conversation with read-only code/tool access and a verdict-specific synthetic output instruction. - Independence boundary: distinct child inference/context and enforced read-only policy; no organizational independence or separate provider required. - Who acts on findings: parent/model or human may initiate repair after receiving the returned report; no first-party mandatory veto/correction obligation is established.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no complete agent-owned prospective external sensing, option development and adopted future-capability return loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: future tool/model/skill changes could require adaptation, but current mechanisms mainly provide session context, user configuration and task-specific retrieval. - Decisive decision or feedback right: main agent can invoke installskill for a supplied local/Git source, but no shipped autonomous prospective adaptation choice or external/future scan is connected to this as an organizational loop. - Decision owner: operator or present-task model for explicit skill install and project preferences, fixed memory extraction/consolidation for session context retention. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: MemoryExtractor, MemoryConsolidator, skill registry, installskill, plugin loading and /skill commands. - Closure path: user/agent may install an existing skill or extract explicit user preferences into memory; no outside-and-future distinction → adaptation alternatives → first-party adoption judgment → changed continuing capability circuit is evidenced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: automatic extraction of user-stated preferences is not future capability selection; a generic model-callable skill installer does not alone own the prospective judgment about which external capabilities the organization should acquire. - Evidence: memory extractor, consolidation, skill install, skills. - Basis: inspected first-party durable-memory/skill paths and their authority boundary. - Confidence: medium for bounded negative; a stronger shipped prospective policy could change classification at a newer pinned ref. - Caveats: skill installation materially changes available runbooks, but source acceptance and requested installation alone are not sufficient evidence for a complete adaptive S4 owner.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no legitimate ultimate identity/policy decision institution and returned governing loop is established for a local Mini-Code-Agent deployment. - Disturbance / variety regulated: ultimate purpose and legitimate cross-objective tradeoffs remain operator/developer choices external to the shipped current runtime governance. - Decisive decision or feedback right: operator sets tactical permissions/model modes and developer rules; no separate highest-policy authority decides identity-level issues and governs later lower-system choices. - Decision owner: user for local tool permissions/identity hints and upstream maintainers for software design, not an installed S5 body. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: PermissionManager, mode rules, OS sandbox, sensitive path restrictions, append-only hash-chain action audit and prompt instructions. - Closure path: action request → allow/ask/deny or audit record → task proceeds/fails; no identity-level policy issue → ultimate authority → return affecting organizational purpose. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: recording tamper-evident tool actions or checking file permission is accountability/enforcement infrastructure, not deciding what the organization is ultimately for. - Evidence: permissions, audit log, app wiring. - Basis: policy/identity and audit runtime paths inspected. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: the operator legitimately governs local work, but shipped controls do not form S5=P without the ultimate-policy loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/c02cac9aa931535a6470913b0fd45adb1c3578cf/assessments/mi/mini-code-agent.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit c02cac9aa931535a6470913b0fd45adb1c3578cf\">c02cac9 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-10T22:08:14+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/XYZ-AI-Lab/AxisAgentic\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AxisAgentic</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-10\">2026-11-10</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/XYZ-AI-Lab/AxisAgentic/tree/0aa54b14307942edc4401831110d8e7f726ec738\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 0aa54b14307942edc4401831110d8e7f726ec738\">0aa54b1 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: an agentic search/research operation transforms a user question and changing web/tool observations into an answer. - Disturbance / variety regulated: missing or inconsistent online evidence, tool errors, large contexts, unexpected search responses and evolving draft answers. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select the next research tool call, read observations, revise the query/answer and decide a final response within configured limits. - Decision owner: configured model-backed task agent operating through TaskOrchestrator and ConversationRuntime. The deterministic runtime limits but does not choose the research plan/content. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: typed message states, tool validation and dispatch, remote client, context compaction/rollback, retry caps and append-only trace. - Closure path: recipe task → model completion → structured search/scrape/code tool action → result returned to model-visible conversation → subsequent model decision or final answer. Optional failure handling can launch another bounded attempt. - Boundary reachability: shipped run configuration invokes the concrete recipe orchestrator derived from the model/tool runtime; it is not just an abstract SDK base class. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing model discretion leaves fixed scheduling and tool/trace infrastructure but no choice of research questions/tools or answer. A provider is externally hosted; the first-party runtime nevertheless wires the executable model-to-tool feedback loop. - Evidence: runtime loop, conversation stage, tool manager. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high for shipped agent-choice/feedback wiring; no real provider execution independently replicated. - Caveats: accuracy, successful external search credentials and longitudinal operational viability are not validated by static review.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no evidenced first-party inter-S1 conflict/oscillation attenuation with a returned coordination decision at the chosen task/recipe recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: concurrently evaluated dataset tasks and nested tool calls may share compute or provider budgets, but they are independent evaluation jobs rather than interacting operational work cells in a sourced conflict-control loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no identified coordination-specific discretion over clashes among distinct autonomous task S1 units. - Decision owner: developer-configured dataset concurrency and deterministic retry/tool execution; model agent decides only its own task/tool calls. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: BatchEvaluator.maxconcurrent, task IDs, tool manager, nested OrchestratorTool and fixed attempt budgets. - Closure path: separate task results are logged and evaluated; no conflict signal from one S1 changes subsequent coordination behaviour of another S1. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: an orchestrator name, concurrent sample scheduler and ability to nest an agent as a tool do not prove a concrete inter-S1 disturbance/attenuation/feedback relation. - Evidence: task orchestration, nested orchestrator tool, dataset evaluator. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high within the shipped recipes. - Caveats: a downstream user could implement genuine multi-agent coordination with these primitives, but it is not credited without the first-party path.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no agent- or parent-owned whole-system current supervisory discretion over a portfolio of S1 resource/commitment choices is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: per-query failure/turn/context limits and batch completion are regulated with fixed configuration, not negotiated whole-current operational commitments. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party supervisor selecting/revising current shared priorities, resources, accountability or interventions using a whole-operating-system view. - Decision owner: human/developer run parameters and deterministic orchestrator/batch scheduler; each agent controls only its local research task. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: model retry/backoff, timeout/turn/tool budgets, batch evaluator concurrency, benchmark dashboard and execution traces. - Closure path: local retry/budget responses remain within one S1 or evaluation job; no whole-current discretionary supervisory intervention feeds back into running S1 cells. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: hard budget enforcement and named orchestration do not own an S3 decision; no supported S3=P human decision-return mode is documented in the first-party recipe. - Evidence: runtime state, orchestrator, batch evaluator. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high in shipped modes. - Caveats: separate human-run experiment governance is not an operationally closed parent S3 mode of the assessed runtime.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: in the supported post-answer self-verification mode, an auxiliary model-backed verifier independently re-investigates the source question and challenges a candidate production answer. - Disturbance / variety regulated: apparently plausible but unsupported/inaccurate research answers can survive ordinary production summaries and tool-use logs. - Claim being audited: the producing research agent&#39;s boxed/final answer to the original web-search question. - Ordinary reporting path: main WebSearchTaskOrchestrator returns its answer and writes model/tool trace; routine retry/format checks do not independently establish answer correctness. - Complementary access path: a separate auxiliary task ID and verification system prompt requests condition-by-condition fact checking with structured web-search tools; the verifier can collect its own observations instead of accepting only the author&#39;s written rationale. - Independence boundary: a new verifier conversation/task context and different verification objective/trace are established by runselfverificationauxiliaryattempt; its model endpoint and tool implementations may be shared, so this is task-context/evidence-search independence, not provider/model independence or a claim of adversarial human audit. - Who acts on findings: maybeapplyselfverification uses the verifier&#39;s correct/incorrect verdict to accept the answer or start a separate re-answer attempt; subsequent verdicts can change the returned result. Deterministic verdict parsing/attempt caps enforce the agent&#39;s judgment, not independently own it. - Decisive decision or feedback right: judge candidate answer against fresh research evidence as correct/incorrect; on rejection, cause re-answer rather than blindly return the original conclusion. - Decision owner: separate model-backed verifier operating within the first-party Web Search recipe, subject to static attempt/format limits. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: dedicated verification prompt/schema, auxiliary trace IDs, web tool manager, verdict parsing/resampling, bounded re-answer, exported trace metadata. - Closure path: original answer → auxiliary independent verification task with tools → model verdict → branch to acceptance or fresh re-answer → verified/revised result returned as the final task output and trace metadata. - Boundary reachability: the distributed Web Search default YAML sets selfverification.enabled: true; config runner passes this flag to benchmark runtime that constructs the dedicated verifier path. Generic class default False is explicitly overridden by this first-party recipe. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the verification model evaluates the original answer using its own research session; fixed verdict parser and retry budget merely enforce its chosen judgment. This differs materially from post-run benchmark scoring or routine answer-format checking. - Evidence: self-verification prompt, separate auxiliary tool run, verdict and re-answer, enabled recipe config, CLI/runner wiring. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high for a shipped separate context/tool-verification return path; actual independence strength and accuracy require empirical testing. - Caveats: verifier uses the candidate model; correlated model/search bias remains possible. The SelfVerificationConfig generic default is disabled and users can turn off the shipped recipe option; S3 is not claimed for every use of the reusable core library.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no supported first-party prospective environmental assessment that selects a reusable future capability adaptation and returns it to current task operation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing live web results are immediate research evidence, while trajectory export and evaluation can inform developer research outside the runtime. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no source-wired runtime actor turns external/future changes into selected and deployed lasting changes to the agent&#39;s own operating repertoire. - Decision owner: developer/operator choices for model, recipes, tools, prompts and experiment setup; agents search to answer current questions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: search tools, context compress/discard, retries, trace/SFT export, evaluator and benchmark dashboard. - Closure path: web evidence changes an answer within S1 or an S3 verdict, not durable runtime future capability; post-run data exports are external research outputs. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: search is outward-looking but task-immediate; trace learning/export is not a closed adaptation decision and implementation loop. - Evidence: architecture split, search recipe, SFT export. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high at the shipped task-agent boundary. - Caveats: externally retraining a model on saved traces is possible but not credited as autonomous S4 here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party ultimate identity/policy authority with a governing decision-return loop at task-agent recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: tool permissions, system prompts, benchmark constraints and token/turn budgets are preset execution boundaries, not constitutional disputes adjudicated in a runtime policy forum. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no evidenced agent or legitimate runtime parent accepting an identity-level issue and deciding an authoritative durable policy that governs subsequent operations. - Decision owner: external user/developer for YAML prompts, credential/tool/endpoint choices and benchmark/task policies. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: prompt templates, strict configuration schemas, tool managers, context/turn limits, sandbox restrictions and run stop rules. - Closure path: tool outputs and verifier judgments influence present answers, not ultimate identity/policy; no corresponding authority-return path for S5. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a static system prompt or nested orchestrator is not a policy-organizational owner; external experiment governance is outside the first-party task agent. - Evidence: config schema, runtime state limits, tool manager. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high within the shipped core/recipe paths. - Caveats: repository maintainers, benchmark owners and model providers can set external policy, but they are not granted a closed S5=P path by the runtime.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/2b84d5d2db11b7358e06f47c53742a805b56bdac/assessments/ax/axisagentic.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 2b84d5d2db11b7358e06f47c53742a805b56bdac\">2b84d5d &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-04T14:58:25+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/QoderAI/better-harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Better Harness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-04\">2026-11-04</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/QoderAI/better-harness/tree/b605532fcf1014bcb48d3e6940a5bc64e80cddd5\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision b605532fcf1014bcb48d3e6940a5bc64e80cddd5\">b605532 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: produce evidence-backed diagnostic findings and, in a separate authorized route, a bounded repair outcome. - Disturbance / variety regulated: divergent project/session/asset evidence, uncertain provenance and differing workflow failure causes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: three specialist AI passes judge candidate findings in distinct lanes; the lead AI decides the final supported findings, severity, score and output narrative. - Decision owner: the host-executed AI specialists and lead, following the first-party packaged Skill. Host inference/tool delivery is support, not an independent Better Harness policy owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: read-only evidence bundle, deterministic analyzers, scope limits, output schema, renderer and revision checks. - Closure path: evidence bundle → specialist results → lead reconciliation and report judgment → validated report and optional bounded repair result. - Boundary reachability: the published Skill explicitly directs host agents to run the three passes and lead reconciliation in ordinary installed host invocation; it is not merely a development example. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: content judgment depends on AI discretion over findings and fixes, not on the JavaScript collectors alone. - Evidence: Skill Steps 1–4, README host routes. - Basis: explicit. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: host inference and permissions must be configured; this is the installed Better Harness review work cell, not an independently booting agent runtime.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no established inter-S1 oscillation-regulation function at this review-cell boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no specific destructive cross-specialist interference is shown; specialists deliberately receive non-overlapping read-only evidence. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none shown for a coordination decision that changes subsequent specialist behavior. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: the Skill&#39;s parallel launch and evidence partitioning are scheduling/isolation, and lead reconciliation is terminal report judgment. - Closure path: no inter-S1 feedback loop beyond collecting independent answers into the lead report. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: parallel execution and deduplicating similar findings do not establish a specific S2 disturbance-and-feedback relationship. - Evidence: independent evidence passes and restrictions. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: this is not a claim that host coding agents can never coordinate in another product mode.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: current whole-report control over the three evidence lanes and acceptance of their work on behalf of the diagnostic task. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incomplete or inconsistent specialist evidence, unsupported claims, scope overruns and duplicate/conflated findings could undermine the whole review outcome. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the lead AI accepts/defer/merges specialist findings, fixes final severity and scores, selects one support track, and stops or narrows publication on evidence failure. - Decision owner: the review&#39;s AI lead; validators enforce the prescribed result structure and the host executes commands. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: bundle lane status, quality gates, findings.json validation, renderer and hard cap on evidence specialists. - Closure path: cross-lane status and findings reach the lead → agent selects admissible current commitments and their priority → only reconciled findings are rendered or execution is stopped. - Boundary reachability: these lead rights are mandatory in Step 3 of the distributed Skill&#39;s normal analysis mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the lead chooses material accept/reject/priority judgments; code guards do not precompute which real findings deserve inclusion. - Whole-system current view: one bundle with three lane states plus lead facts, then all specialist candidates at reconciliation. - Current-control decision scope: acceptance, severity, support-track and report-level commitment—not control of the operator&#39;s unrelated coding tasks. - Evidence: Skill Steps 2–4, findings quality gate. - Basis: explicit. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: the whole-system boundary is the review cell; report acceptance should not be confused with general parent governance of all coding agents.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: ? - Function: a plausible independent post-fix check of a repair claim, but the complementary-access boundary is not fully established from the shipped instructions alone. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the repair-performing lead may overclaim that a reported finding has been fixed. - Decisive decision or feedback right: a fresh read-only agent may judge verified, partial or blocked from the pre-fix finding, actual outputs, changed paths, native validation and refreshed integrity envelope. - Decision owner: a separately instantiated agent for the post-fix verdict, when supported; the deterministic recorder stores it. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: versioned finding lock, target topology binding, asset integrity collector and unchanged pre-fix baseline. - Closure path: independent verdict can update Repair Progress, while efficacy scores require later comparable episodes. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: an AI verdict exists, but the reviewer receives lead-provided bounded inputs, is forbidden to rescan the project, and can be omitted on delegation failure; it is unresolved whether its evidence access is materially complementary to ordinary repair reporting. - Evidence: finding-bound repair / Reassess the Repair Independently. - Basis: explicit for the route, unknown for independent complementary access. - Confidence: low. - Caveats: no independent S3 audit is inferred from ordinary specialist decomposition, validators or host subagent naming alone.\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: P - Function: prepare prospective improvements to the project-facing coding-agent workflow, with operator-controlled adoption of a selected repair. - Disturbance / variety regulated: evolving project needs, repeated session friction, missing rules/assets and changing operational evidence can make the existing workflow ineffective. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the lead develops prioritized options; the parent operator selects and invokes an actual repair against an authorized project/finding, delimiting the adoption decision. - Decision owner: operator/parent for the adoption decision; AI lead for option formation and scoped repair implementation, with deterministic tools checking target/expected revision. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: session/project/asset evidence collectors, repair callback, workspace topology and revision guards. - Closure path: observed project/session mismatches → agent-generated future improvements → operator invokes a finding-bound repair → host AI edits the authorized target → validators and recorded outputs inform later work. - Boundary reachability: both diagnostic findings with repair actions and callback-based bounded repair are first-party installed Skill routes; no external repository maintainer workflow is borrowed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the agent generates prospective options but the decisive commitment to change the live work environment is parent-selected in the evidenced finding-bound route. - External distinction: observed project changes, session outcomes and host agent assets rather than only an internal current subtask plan. - Future / prospective distinction: findings specify an improved workflow/asset and verifiable future operating outcome. - Adaptation option generated: bounded project/agent-asset repair and a prioritized expected output. - Path back into current capability / S3: applied local instructions/assets and recorded repair status influence subsequent host-agent sessions; later comparable task episodes, not same-run scores, measure effectiveness. - Evidence: README workflow and improvement route, Skill Step 3/5, finding-bound repair. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: reports alone are proposals; no automatic adoption, causal improvement or unattended future sensing is claimed. This P is a supported operator-selected adaptation path, not generic task approval.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no shipped operational identity/ultimate-policy adjudication loop at this review-cell recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: user-selected scope, risk and permission restrictions remain outer constraints, not an identified unresolved identity tension. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no such in-boundary right evidenced. - Decision owner: no agentic or first-party parent S5 closure established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Skill&#39;s fixed guardrails, stop conditions, permission scoping and CLI validation. - Closure path: no identity-level issue → legitimate parent decision → returned policy path is supplied; ordinary human choice of report scope or fix does not qualify. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: static instructions constrain review actions without giving an actor an ultimate-policy decision process. - Evidence: Skill scope and guardrails, repair authority boundary. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: product developers may make policy decisions in a separate repository-governance system, excluded from this installed review cell.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/8671f5335902711f29462b3262914236a47f2cda/assessments/be/better-harness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 8671f5335902711f29462b3262914236a47f2cda\">8671f53 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"2\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-22T21:07:39+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/risa-labs-inc/BossConsole\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">BossConsole</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>2</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-22\">2026-09-22</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/risa-labs-inc/BossConsole/tree/d4db04882425327915e0520a366eac2da8af01f3\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision d4db04882425327915e0520a366eac2da8af01f3\">d4db048 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform bounded software-engineering and other tool-mediated work in the relevant project/environment through an attached external AI coding-agent session. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous user objectives, project/repository state, terminal/browser/file feedback, tool results, model uncertainty, external services and task-local failures. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose reasoning, tool invocations and concrete task actions within the admitted objective and BOSS-enforced authority envelope. - Decision owner: the autonomous Claude Code, Codex, Gemini or OpenCode/model actor attached through the first-party BOSS terminal/MCP path. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: BossTerm, agent attachment/re-attachment, MCP registry, terminal/browser/editor/file/git tools, secrets, automation, RBAC/policy/approval and per-tool kill switches. - Closure path: objective is given to an attached coding agent → the agent reasons and chooses BOSS/MCP actions → BOSS transports/enforces those calls → project/tool/environment feedback returns to the agent → the agent continues until the bounded outcome is produced. - Boundary reachability: the pinned README documents launching the supported CLIs in BOSS terminals, attaching them to the local BOSS MCP server and automatically re-attaching them across restarts/port changes; this is a normal installed operating path rather than repository CI or dogfood infrastructure. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: BOSS constrains and enables the work, but the substantive local task decisions remain with the external autonomous agent. Policy enforcement does not transfer S1 ownership to the deterministic host. - Evidence: README.md; docs/CLI.md; composeApp/src/commonMain/kotlin/ai/rever/boss/mcp/WorkspaceMcpToolProvider.kt. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the external agent runtime is credited only as the autonomous actor reached through BOSS&#39;s standard distribution; its internal harness functions are not inherited into BossConsole.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: prevent destructive interference when multiple sibling coding-agent evolution sessions work on different changes to the same plugin repository in parallel. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two or more evolution agents operating in one source checkout can overwrite or mix one another&#39;s edits/branch state while independently changing the same plugin. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the isolation/coordination relation under which concurrent evolution work is separated into dedicated worktrees/branches rather than sharing one checkout. - Decision owner: the reviewed standard distribution does not package an autonomous S2 coordinator for this choice. The first-party Tool Evolver exposes an S2-specific worktree mode and allocator; an autonomous owner would still have to be composed over that path. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: EvolveMode.WORKTREE, ensureWorktree, dedicated evolve/&lt;slug&gt; branches, separate working directories, tracked evolution sessions and branch-specific generated skill guidance. - Closure path: several bounded evolution sessions target the same plugin → Worktree mode establishes a dedicated &lt;repo&gt;/.worktrees/&lt;slug&gt; checkout and evolve/&lt;slug&gt; branch per change → the selected CLI agent is launched with that isolated directory/branch → its subsequent file/git behaviour occurs in the separated worktree instead of clobbering sibling work. - Boundary reachability: the worktree mode is a shipped Tool Evolver UI/runtime path in the first-party companion explicitly integrated by BOSS; it is not a CI-only repository convention. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the runtime mechanically creates and enforces the chosen worktree relation, but no autonomous actor in the standard path owns the decision to select that coordination mode. The primitive is nevertheless S2-specific because its own documentation states that it exists so several feature/issue evolutions for the same plugin can run in parallel without clobbering each other. - Evidence: EvolveLauncher.kt; EvolverTabViewModel.kt. - Basis: structural + explicit intent comment. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: generic JVM multithreading, multiple terminals, multiple attached agents and workspace plurality are not credited as S2. The positive mapping is limited to the concrete same-plugin parallel-evolution interference witness and its dedicated worktree relation. - Distinct S1 units: separate external coding-agent evolution sessions launched in separate BossTerm tabs, each responsible for a bounded feature/issue change to the same plugin. - Inter-S1 disturbance: concurrent sessions sharing one plugin checkout can clobber one another&#39;s source/git state; EvolveLauncher explicitly defines worktree mode so several features/issues can be evolved in parallel without clobbering each other. - Attenuating coordination relation: one dedicated git worktree and evolve/&lt;slug&gt; branch per evolution session/change. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: the selected coding agent is launched with the isolated worktree as its working directory and receives branch guidance requiring commits/PR work on that dedicated branch. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the relation is tied to a named concrete interference mode between sibling operational agents over the same mutable repository, and it changes their subsequent filesystem/git context specifically to prevent that interference.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: C(P) - Function: regulate the running BOSS installation as a whole by observing current workspace/tool/plugin health and deciding which current workspaces, terminals, capabilities and agent tool rights remain active. - Disturbance / variety regulated: current workspaces can be active/stopped, plugins can fail or become unhealthy, MCP calls can be denied/failed, and tool access or current agent resource use may need intervention on behalf of the whole installation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose current interventions such as opening/closing workspaces or terminals, enabling/disabling agent tools, setting current MCP policy/approval decisions, and acting on installation health/recovery findings. - Decision owner: base constructor mode — BOSS intentionally exposes structured whole-installation state and current-control operations to AI/automation clients, but does not package a distinct autonomous whole-system controller that owns those choices. Parent mode — the BOSS operator using the shipped workspace, Toolbox/MCP policy, kill-switch and health/recovery controls. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: boss status --json, boss doctor, MCP registry/list/describe/invoke, WorkspaceMcpToolProvider, policy engine, approval UI, per-tool kill switch, recent MCP activity ledger, plugin watchdog and health/recovery surfaces. - Closure path: current installation/workspace/tool health and activity is observed → controller/operator chooses a present intervention → BOSS workspace/MCP/policy/plugin machinery applies it → exposed tools/workspaces/terminals or plugin state change → subsequent agent operation proceeds under the revised current state. - Boundary reachability: the CLI agent harness and workspace lifecycle MCP provider are packaged runtime interfaces; operator MCP policy/kill-switch and recovery controls are normal desktop surfaces. No maintainer or repository-development action is required for the claimed current-control paths. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: BOSS exposes enough function-specific state and authority for an autonomous controller to be composed, but ordinary attached coding agents are not documented as the installation&#39;s whole-system S3 owner. Deterministic policy/watchdog/lifecycle machinery enforces decisions rather than supplying that discretion. The operator mode does close the S3 decision and return path. - Evidence: docs/CLI.md; WorkspaceMcpToolProvider.kt; README.md; docs/release-notes/v9.5.19.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: individual approvals, process lifecycle, health checks and tool toggles are not promoted separately to S3; the positive mapping relies on their combination with installation-wide/current workspace/tool state and authority to change subsequent operation. - Whole-system current view: boss status --json/boss doctor report the running instance, active project and cross-area plugin/browser/MCP health; listworkspaces reports all existing workspaces with active/running status; the MCP registry and recent activity view expose the current governed tool plane and recent policy outcomes. - Current-control decision scope: open/create/close workspaces and terminals, change which tools an agent may call through policy/kill switches, and perform current plugin recovery/intervention based on health findings.\">C(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no material first-party complementary organizational audit path is established for independently challenging an S1 producer&#39;s operational claim and returning findings into current control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: BOSS exposes health checks, activity/audit records, plugin signatures, security validation and Tool Evolver probe/verification, but these surfaces do not by themselves create an independent organizational challenge to ordinary S1 reporting. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for an independent audit judgment over an S1 claim. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: boss doctor, health/watchdog reporting, MCP operation/activity ledgers, signature/integrity checks, policy validation, Tool Evolver memory/log probes and the evolve skill&#39;s self-verification step were inspected but are not credited as S3 ownership. - Closure path: not applicable for the negative finding. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the reviewed runtime supplies telemetry, deterministic integrity/security checks and producer-controlled/self-invoked verification, but no separate autonomous auditor with materially complementary access whose adverse finding is returned into current control. - Evidence: docs/CLI.md; README.md; evolve-skill-body.md. - Basis: explicit + structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: repository CI/Claude review workflows are adjacent development organization and excluded from the installed harness boundary. Tool Evolver verification is called by the same adaptation agent loop and therefore does not supply organizational independence.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A(P) - Function: adapt the BOSS tool/capability repertoire when current or anticipated user/task/environment requirements expose a capability change worth implementing. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing project/user requirements and tool behaviour can make an installed capability insufficient, faulty or suboptimal; the system must develop and test a revised capability for subsequent work without taking the running harness offline. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose which installed plugin/capability should be evolved and what adaptation request should be pursued, then accept the resulting live-loaded change for continued use in the running BOSS instance. - Decision owner: autonomous base mode — an authorized attached agent can inspect available tools/probe evidence and invoke the first-party evolverevolve MCP tool itself with a selected plugin, agent and optional evolution task; the spawned coding agent owns implementation choices and applies/verifies the changed capability through evolverhotreload. Parent mode — the human operator selects the plugin, requested change and evolve action through the first-party Tool Evolver UI. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: evolverlisttools, evolverprobe, permission-gated evolverevolve, source-repository discovery/clone, generated evolve skill, build tooling, evolverhotreload, live plugin loader, probe/log verification, evolution session tracking and PR creation. - Closure path: external/user/task requirement or observed capability shortfall reaches an authorized agent/operator → a plugin and evolution task are selected → Tool Evolver launches a coding agent against the plugin source → the agent implements/builds → the resulting jar is hot-reloaded into the running BOSS instance → evolverprobe/logs verify the live capability and the agent iterates as needed → subsequent BOSS operation uses the changed tool; a PR is then opened for upstream persistence. - Boundary reachability: the pinned host README advertises Tool Evolver as a supported first-party Toolbox component and explicitly says an agent can reshape a tool while the app remains running; the pinned host contains the runtime Evolver MCP contract, and the contemporaneous first-party plugin exposes the agent-facing evolve/hot-reload tools used by that shipped path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: unlike generic plugin hot-reload or self-improvement naming, the base mode gives an autonomous agent the actual adaptation decision/action path: select a target and task, launch evolution, implement, live-load, observe and iterate. Deterministic loader/build machinery supports that decision. A separate operator mode lets the parent own the adaptation request instead. - Evidence: README.md; EvolverContract.kt; Tool Evolver README; ToolEvolverMcpTools.kt; evolve-skill-body.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: internal memory/leak/log probing alone would not establish S4. The positive finding depends on the complete future-capability loop and on an external/user/task distinction supplying the adaptation need. The A claim is bounded to runtime capability of the installed BOSS instance; opening a PR does not give the agent ultimate authority over upstream maintainer merge/release governance. - External distinction: the adaptation request arises from user/project/task requirements in BOSS&#39;s environment, and Tool Evolver can acquire the target plugin&#39;s external source repository; the system is not merely consolidating internal memory or replaying a prior run. - Future / prospective distinction: the requested evolution is explicitly aimed at changing the tool so subsequent agent work has a different capability; the evolve loop tests the revised plugin live before upstream persistence. - Adaptation option generated: an authorized agent/operator selects a target and evolution task; Tool Evolver launches an AI coding agent that develops a concrete plugin modification on an evolution branch/worktree. - Path back into current capability / S3: evolverhotreload copies the newly built jar into the running host&#39;s plugin directory, unloads/loads it without restart, and the evolve skill probes/verifies the live result before subsequent operation continues.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material first-party identity/ultimate-policy closure path is established at the running BOSS recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: BOSS has extensive RBAC, per-tool policy, approvals, secrets, role/admin semantics, plugin signing and capability-evolution controls, but these regulate operational authority, current control, security and adaptation rather than deciding what BOSS ultimately is or which identity-defining policy prevails. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for an identity/ultimate-policy issue. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: server-side RBAC/RLS, admin permission semantics, MCP allow/ask/deny policy, per-tool kill switch, secrets, signed plugins, operator approvals, plugin install/update/evolution and external upstream PR governance were inspected but are not promoted to S5. - Closure path: not applicable for the negative finding. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: there is no established S5 function to assign. Operator supremacy over ordinary permissions or capability changes is not itself identity closure, and the agent&#39;s ability to evolve tools remains S4 rather than ultimate policy. - Evidence: README.md; docs/release-notes/v9.5.19.md; docs/CLI.md. - Basis: explicit + structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: strong security/governance controls do not become S5 without an identity/ultimate-policy issue, legitimate ultimate authority for that issue and a return-to-operation path.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/f04815ff8e03993c24c7ad49d4093feb09462a74/assessments/bossconsole.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit f04815ff8e03993c24c7ad49d4093feb09462a74\">f04815f &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-23T00:15:15+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/marcelsud/claude-foreman\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Foreman</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-22\">2026-09-22</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/marcelsud/claude-foreman/tree/1863451a47f914a909f2b3857a73e87645ccd1fe\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 1863451a47f914a909f2b3857a73e87645ccd1fe\">1863451 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform bounded implementation work in a target repository through a background Claude Code or Codex worker operating in a Foreman-created worktree. - Disturbance / variety regulated: task-specific repository state, implementation uncertainty, tests/build feedback, local tool outcomes, clarifying questions and review feedback from earlier attempts. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the substantive task-local reasoning path, edits and tool actions required to satisfy the assigned implementation prompt within Foreman&#39;s worktree/sandbox/approval envelope. - Decision owner: the autonomous external Claude/Codex worker actor launched through Foreman&#39;s first-party runner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: provider/model/effort assignment, worker policy, worktree lifecycle, sandboxing, turn budget, exact approval hooks, cancellation, durable events and retry/requeue lifecycle. - Closure path: manager admits a task → detached scheduler claims it → Foreman prepares an isolated worktree and launches the selected external worker → worker makes task-local decisions and receives repository/tool feedback → worker reports completion/failure → Foreman runs verification and returns the result to metasystem review; requeue feedback can enter a later worker attempt. - Boundary reachability: background task creation and execution are the primary shipped product path documented in the repository README and developer reference; runner.py directly launches Claude/Codex worker integrations under the normal daemon workflow. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the first-party control plane owns admission, lifecycle and constraints, but the model actor owns substantive implementation reasoning. Foreman&#39;s deterministic scheduler, sandbox and policy hooks do not choose the code-level solution. - Evidence: README.md; plugins/foreman/README.md; plugins/foreman/src/foreman/runner.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the internal organizational functions of Claude Code/Codex are not inherited. The positive state credits the reachable external operational actor only for the task-local decision right Foreman actually delegates to it.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no installation-level S2 mutual-adjustment function is established from the reviewed standard distribution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Foreman can run multiple tasks, isolate them in worktrees, sequence dependency-gated phases and keep a shared-worktree workflow history linear, but the inspected standard paths do not establish a concrete sibling-generated oscillation/interference signal together with a coordination decision/feedback loop whose function is to damp that disturbance between active S1 units. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established at the declared recursion. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: per-task worktrees, dependency records, atomic ready-task claiming, reviewed workflow phase gates, priority and worker-concurrency limits provide isolation/sequencing/resource structure without independently satisfying S2. - Closure path: not applicable for the negative finding. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: no qualifying S2 function was established, so there is no S2-specific decision right to classify. - Evidence: plugins/foreman/README.md; plugins/foreman/skills/manage-foreman-agents/SKILL.md; plugins/foreman/skills/manage-foreman-agents/references/workflow-schema.md; plugins/foreman/src/foreman/database.py. - Basis: explicit + structural negative search. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: dependency scheduling can be part of S2 in another boundary if evidence ties it to a concrete inter-S1 disturbance. Here the documentation states dependency/prerequisite and linear-history semantics, not a distinct sensed sibling disturbance and corrective mutual-adjustment loop. Separate worktrees are preventive isolation rather than sufficient S2 evidence by themselves.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: regulate the current Foreman organization by deciding task admission/configuration, routine approvals, responses to actionable worker events, review/requeue/accept outcomes and cancellation/goal state while preserving human authority over critical actions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: queued/running/blocked tasks, approval requests, worker errors, failed verification, incomplete diffs, model/provider choices, dependency readiness, cancellations and risky actions can require whole-goal current intervention. - Decisive decision or feedback right: base mode — the autonomous managing Claude/Codex session chooses task definitions/configuration, routine exact approvals, monitoring responses, review feedback, requeue or acceptance; parent mode — the human user decides human-only critical approval requests and can direct/cancel/withhold acceptance through the manager interface. - Decision owner: base mode — the autonomous external managing assistant explicitly assigned the manager role by the shipped Foreman skill and MCP interface. Parent mode — the human user for critical actions and direct supervisory decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: compact task/goal views, actionable taskwait wake events, durable event/approval state, exact hash-bound approval API, scheduler state, task configure/cancel, goal status, task diff, verification summary, requeue/accept transitions and daemon controls. - Closure path: Foreman exposes current goal/task/approval/verification state → managing assistant or human identifies a present-control issue → decision is made through first-party MCP controls → scheduler/runner/database changes current task/approval/goal state → durable events/wake surfaces return the new current state to the manager. - Boundary reachability: the root README instructs users to install the plugin into Claude Code/Codex and start a manager session; the shipped skill explicitly says the managing session is responsible for scope, approvals and final review, and mcpserver.py exposes the corresponding current-control tools. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the autonomous manager path is function-specific rather than inherited from generic model capability: the shipped skill instructs it to obtain an operational snapshot, wait on actionable events, decide scoped approvals, inspect diffs, requeue defects and accept complete work through Foreman&#39;s MCP tools. Deterministic scheduler/state transitions enforce decisions but do not own them. Human-only categories establish a distinct parent-governed mode. - Evidence: README.md; plugins/foreman/skills/manage-foreman-agents/SKILL.md; plugins/foreman/skills/manage-foreman-agents/references/approval-policy.md; plugins/foreman/src/foreman/mcpserver.py; plugins/foreman/src/foreman/database.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: queue claiming, dependency release, wake IPC, turn budgets and lifecycle state machines are supporting mechanisms and are not counted as autonomous S3 merely because they supervise execution. The autonomous state is grounded in the shipped manager role plus reachable current-control tools. - Whole-system current view: tasklist/taskget, goal state, actionable event cursors, approval lists, verification summaries, usage and the dashboard expose current task status, worker/model, progress, approvals, dependencies, verification and worktree details across the Foreman-managed goal. - Current-control decision scope: create/configure/cancel tasks; set goal state; decide routine approvals; respond to worker questions; review full diffs/verification; requeue with feedback or accept; start/stop scheduler; escalate human-only risk.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: independently test the worker-produced repository state through declared verification gates and return that complementary evidence into review/rework before acceptance. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a worker can report completion while tests/lint/build/read-only Git checks fail, the verification environment errors, or the reviewed snapshot differs from what was actually tested. - Decisive decision or feedback right: define the verification criteria/commands whose independently executed results will challenge the worker&#39;s ordinary completion report before current control accepts or requeues the result. - Decision owner: Foreman does not package an autonomous auditor that chooses the audit criteria. The project/managing assistant supplies verificationcommands (or composes an explicit reviewer phase); Foreman supplies the dedicated independent execution, fingerprinting, persistence and feedback path. Therefore the S3 path is constructor-owned. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: separate verification App Server process, argv-only command execution, workspace-write sandbox, network disabled, bounded output/timeout, worktree fingerprint, durable gate/result records, verifying state, actionable verification-failure events, full diff view and review/requeue/accept transitions. - Closure path: worker finishes and supplies its ordinary result summary → Foreman moves task to verifying → configured gates execute independently against the worker worktree and produce pass/fail/error plus tested-state fingerprint → task moves to awaitingreview with verification summary → manager inspects diff/evidence → failed/incomplete work can be taskrequeued with concrete feedback → Foreman persists review.feedback and injects it into the next worker prompt; accepted work closes the task. - Boundary reachability: verificationcommands are normal task/workflow inputs exposed by the shipped MCP surface and explicitly recommended by the manager skill; runner.py invokes runverificationgates on every finished task that has gates before review. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the verification service is organizationally separate from the worker self-report and its evidence is tied to the tested worktree snapshot, but the meaningful audit judgment is configured by the manager/project rather than selected by an autonomous first-party reviewer. The manager&#39;s later accept/requeue decision is S3 response to audit evidence, not transfer of S3 audit ownership. - Evidence: README.md; plugins/foreman/skills/manage-foreman-agents/SKILL.md; plugins/foreman/src/foreman/verification.py; plugins/foreman/src/foreman/runner.py; plugins/foreman/src/foreman/database.py; plugins/foreman/skills/manage-foreman-agents/references/workflow-schema.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a reviewed workflow may explicitly add a separate Claude/Codex reviewer phase, but that phase is downstream-composed rather than a mandatory autonomous auditor supplied by Foreman. Its existence reinforces constructor capability rather than changing the standalone state to A. - Claim being audited: the implementation worker&#39;s claim that its finished worktree/result is ready to advance to acceptance. - Ordinary reporting path: external worker completes its task and returns a textual result/summary to runner.py. - Complementary access path: Foreman&#39;s separate verification service executes the configured repository commands directly against the finished worktree and computes a fingerprint of the tested tracked patch plus untracked files. - Independence boundary: verification runs through a distinct Codex App Server command process after worker execution, under its own sandbox/network/output/timeout rules; gate results do not depend on the worker&#39;s self-reported test summary. - Who acts on findings: the managing assistant receives actionable failed-verification/review state, inspects gate output/fingerprint and full diff, then accepts or requeues. Requeue feedback is persisted and returned to the subsequent worker attempt.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no installation-level S4 outside-and-then adaptation loop is established from the reviewed standard distribution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Foreman tracks durable event/usage history, provider/model availability, task outcomes and workflow versions, but the inspected paths do not establish environmental sensing with a prospective future distinction that generates an adaptation option and returns it into present capability/S3. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for S4 at the declared recursion. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: durable SQLite history, usage summaries, live provider/model readiness checks, workflow versioning, task reconfiguration while queued and manager-authored workflow proposals. - Closure path: not applicable for the negative finding. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: operational history and current provider availability can inform a manager, but persistence/monitoring/configuration are not a packaged S4 environmental adaptation conversation by themselves. - Evidence: README.md; plugins/foreman/README.md; plugins/foreman/src/foreman/database.py; plugins/foreman/skills/manage-foreman-agents/SKILL.md. - Basis: explicit + structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a user can ask the external manager to research future options, but generic model capability or ad-hoc prompting is not a standard first-party S4 loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no installation-level identity/ultimate-policy closure is established from the reviewed standard distribution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Foreman contains strong safety policy, subscription-only authentication, exact approvals, human-only risk classes, immutable workflow review and explicit no-merge/no-deploy defaults, but these regulate operational authority and risk rather than organizational identity/ultimate policy. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for an identity/ultimate-policy issue. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: approval policy, humanconfirmed critical-action gate, worker policy, sandbox defaults, workflow activation review, model/provider selection and goal status. - Closure path: not applicable for the negative finding. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: human authority over destructive/external actions is substantial parent governance, but generic final say over operational risk does not satisfy S5 without an identity/ultimate-policy issue and return-to-operation closure at that level. - Evidence: README.md; plugins/foreman/skills/manage-foreman-agents/references/approval-policy.md; plugins/foreman/skills/manage-foreman-agents/references/workflow-schema.md; plugins/foreman/src/foreman/mcpserver.py. - Basis: explicit + structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the negative result does not mean Foreman lacks policy. It keeps safety/approval/workflow policy mapped to the operational functions they actually regulate rather than promoting them to S5 from vocabulary or human authority alone.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/6d249c0ac2f32c3b180cdab2e30e55899d54ba9c/assessments/foreman.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 6d249c0ac2f32c3b180cdab2e30e55899d54ba9c\">6d249c0 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-17T21:48:54+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Prism-Shadow/penguin-harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PenguinHarness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-17\">2026-09-17</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Prism-Shadow/penguin-harness/tree/77c746954f92a588295de1c2dad362b1b8593f19\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 77c746954f92a588295de1c2dad362b1b8593f19\">77c7469 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: A. A configured target agent autonomously performs task work through the Penguin runtime. Its model/tool loop owns the next operational action inside the configured task boundary.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: —. Target, evaluator and optimizer are stages in an evaluation/optimization pipeline, not multiple operational S1 units whose mutual interference is regulated by an S2 function. Parallel evaluation cells and subagent calls are execution structure, not sufficient evidence of S2.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: —. The optimizer changes durable future Agent State after evaluations. That is not a separate whole-system current regulator with authority over shared organizational resources, commitments, priorities or constraints. The historical A promoted optimization authority into S3.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: A. The evaluator is deliberately separated from the target&#39;s normal production path: the target receives the public Statement but cannot access the private Rubric/Gold/scoring rules, while the evaluator later inspects the isolated workspace, bound root/child traces and the private Rubric to make its own score judgment. That score enters the optimizer&#39;s accept-or-rollback decision, changing subsequent retained Agent State. This establishes a first-party complementary audit channel with autonomous evaluator ownership.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: —. The optimizer searches improvements against a frozen benchmark using scores and traces from the target&#39;s own task executions. Durable self-improvement is real, but the reviewed path does not establish the required outside-and-then environmental intelligence relation; optimization/learning alone is not S4.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: —. The target purpose, benchmark, desired score and optimization initiation are parent-authored constraints. They do not form a runtime identity/ultimate-policy tension-resolution loop with legitimate S5 authority. Parent configuration therefore does not imply P.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/911ebc94216226df3120d8a5762944319ae24529/assessments/penguin-harness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 911ebc94216226df3120d8a5762944319ae24529\">911ebc9 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-06T17:46:29+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/KlaatAI/klaatcode\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Klaat Code</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-06\">2026-10-06</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/KlaatAI/klaatcode/tree/b988be7cf6ffe7878e87ed3e720759e0f7247c33\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision b988be7cf6ffe7878e87ed3e720759e0f7247c33\">b988be7 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously inspect, modify and verify a software project through model-selected coding/tool actions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unfamiliar code, implementation choices, tool/process/test failures, context pressure, permissions, provider/model variation and bounded delegated subtasks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose substantive coding/tool actions, decide whether to delegate, interpret returned evidence and determine when the task is complete. - Decision owner: the active model-backed Klaat Code agent; write-capable build/general subagents own their bounded delegated work. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tool registry, permissions/sandbox, diagnostics/verification, Plan/Build mode, context compaction, phase/cost guards, sessions, provider routing and hooks/plugins/MCP. - Closure path: user request → model selects direct action or delegated work → first-party runtime executes → results return to the model → model revises/validates or completes. - Boundary reachability: ordinary REPL/headless/server paths instantiate the first-party model/tool loop; \\delegatetask\\ is a built-in model-callable tool. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic runtime code constrains and transports execution, but the model owns open-ended engineering choices and interpretation. - Evidence: README.md; src/screens/repl.ts; src/tools/index.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: hosted Klaatu may perform additional routing, but no hidden hosted function is imported into this public-client assessment.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material first-party inter-S1 coordination loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: concurrent write-capable delegated agents can potentially overlap in one project workspace, but no implemented public-client mechanism is shown detecting/attenuating that peer interference. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S2. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: persona tool scoping, permission prompts, atomic multi-file patch application and read-only explore/review personas constrain individual operations but do not coordinate peer write-capable S1s. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S2 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the main agent may choose multiple delegated tasks, but delegation/concurrency is not itself coordination; roadmap worktree isolation cannot be credited as current runtime behavior. - Evidence: src/agent/personas.ts; src/screens/repl.ts; README.md. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: read-only personas are intentionally parallel-safe; that prevents one class of interference by capability restriction but does not establish a general S2 loop for the write-capable operational units.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no material whole-system current-control function is established over the live set of delegated operational commitments. - Disturbance / variety regulated: background child status is observable, but no standard actor has a substantive live control right over individual current child commitments beyond initial delegation and later result use. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S3. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: \\taskstatus\\, live output tails, completion notices, loop limits, phase/cost stops and whole-turn cancellation expose/enforce state without supplying selective live child reallocation. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S3 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the parent agent can spawn and observe children but cannot kill, steer, retry or reprioritize a specific running background child through the reviewed tool surface. - Evidence: src/screens/repl.ts; src/tools/index.ts. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: task visibility is necessary but insufficient; generic cost/loop stops and turn cancellation are enforcement/safeguards rather than whole-system current-control judgment.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently inspect a coding claim/change through a separate fresh read-only review agent and return findings to the caller. - Disturbance / variety regulated: bugs, edge cases, security issues, needless complexity and defects missed by the authoring agent&#39;s ordinary coding path. - Decisive decision or feedback right: inspect repository evidence independently and produce severity-ranked findings or a clean judgment. - Decision owner: the dedicated model-backed \\review\\ persona instantiated as a fresh delegated agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: isolated subagent context, read-only tool allowlist, dedicated review system prompt, separate routing tier/loop cap and final-report return to the parent. - Closure path: main agent has a code/change claim → invokes \\delegatetask\\ with \\agent: &quot;review&quot;\\ and a self-contained target → fresh reviewer reads/searches repository evidence → reviewer returns findings → parent agent receives the final report and decides corrective/acceptance action. - Boundary reachability: \\review\\ is an explicit built-in persona and an enumerated value of the model-callable \\delegatetask\\ tool. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the audit judgment is produced by a separate model context with direct repository-reading tools, not by deterministic linting or the author merely reviewing its own transcript. - Claim being audited: a specified implementation/change or coding claim supplied by the main agent to the review subagent. - Ordinary reporting path: the main/build agent&#39;s own tool results and completion statement. - Complementary access path: the review persona independently uses read/search/code-graph/web-read tools in a fresh context. - Independence boundary: isolated context, dedicated reviewer prompt, read-only capability boundary, no nested delegation and separate model loop. - Who acts on findings: the parent coding agent receives the reviewer final report and owns subsequent repair or acceptance. - Evidence: src/agent/personas.ts; src/tools/index.ts; src/screens/repl.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the slash \\/review\\ path is not needed for this claim; S3 rests on the dedicated fresh delegated reviewer with direct evidence access.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party external-and-prospective adaptation loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no qualifying changing external/future condition is put under an owner that develops adaptation options and returns them into current organizational capability. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S4. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: automatic/session memory distillation, session resume, code graph, web tools, model switching, skills/plugins/MCP and update checks retain or extend capability but do not by themselves close S4. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S4 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: memory is distilled and injected into later sessions, but persistence/reuse of learned facts is not evidence of an external-and-prospective option-development loop returning into current S3 capability. - Evidence: src/agent/memory.ts; src/screens/repl.ts; README.md. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: hosted Klaatu routing cannot donate an unobserved S4 function to the public client.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material first-party identity/ultimate-policy governance loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no identity-level policy issue is routed to an authoritative owner and returned as governing policy. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S5. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: permission rules, sandbox restrictions, project rules, system/persona prompts, config, hooks and Plan approval constrain execution without constituting identity governance. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S5 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: coding/review agents operate within user/developer-authored rules and have no authority to redefine Klaat Code&#39;s ultimate identity or governing principles. - Evidence: src/permissions/index.ts; src/agent/personas.ts; README.md. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: user approval in Plan/permission flows is operational authority, not an identity/ultimate-policy decision.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/aeb6ed9e6d0d5caddf0fa3797327d1d0613d81af/assessments/klaat-code.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit aeb6ed9e6d0d5caddf0fa3797327d1d0613d81af\">aeb6ed9 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-30T23:22:36+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/KhanUzeb/AXIOM\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AXIOM</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-30\">2026-10-30</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/KhanUzeb/AXIOM/tree/ba3a9bddce05990593035eab635eee9f45c46ff5\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision ba3a9bddce05990593035eab635eee9f45c46ff5\">ba3a9bd &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: specialist agents solve and revise the requested STEM or document-grounded task using calculations, code, research, retrieval and reasoning. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous user problems, unknown equations/constants, failed code, incomplete or inconsistent intermediate results and missing evidence. - Decisive decision or feedback right: model-backed coordinator selects work cells; model-backed specialists choose derivations, executable code, research/retrieval and follow-up work from task and returned results. - Decision owner: first-party wired model agents in the full graph; providers supply inference but do not choose the repository&#39;s organizational wiring, and deterministic tools execute selected acts. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: LangGraph Send and reducer state, SymPy, code-exec timeout/static blocklist, Tavily, local RAG, provider fallback, memory and bounded hops. - Closure path: original query and intermediate observations → coordinator/specialist choice → computation or retrieval → returned result/critic suggestions → further specialist choice or assembled answer. - Boundary reachability: app/api/main.py constructs graph=buildgraph() and invokes graph.ainvoke/astreamevents for non-fast-path queries; buildgraph.py wires the specialists and return edge. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: first-party model calls choose actual solver/code/research work and adapt to tool results; deterministic school calculations are a separate bypass and are not credited as autonomous work. - Evidence: API graph invocation, graph wiring, coordinator, specialists. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high for source-wired agent rights; task success was not measured. - Caveats: provider and hosted tool dependencies were not invoked; the static code blocklist is explicitly not a hardened sandbox.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate actual shared-state write collisions and input-dependency conflicts among concurrently dispatched STEM specialist cells. - Disturbance / variety regulated: multiple Send branches write the same LangGraph superstep (InvalidUpdateError without reducers), can overwrite state, and code dispatched beside math/chemistry cannot see those same-hop prerequisites. - Decisive decision or feedback right: first-party mergedicts, keeplaststatus and narrow specialistpayload prevent update collisions; stageddependentagents schedules a bounded later code hop only after solver results become visible. - Decision owner: developer-defined deterministic S2-specific reducers/staging rules; model coordinator chooses possible parallel workers but does not own the decisive shared-write arbitration, so autonomous S2 authority is not established for this collision. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: LangGraph reducer annotations, unique specialist result keys, one-shot staged-code marker and maxagenthops guard. - Closure path: concurrent specialist writes → reducer-preserved merged results → next coordinator state → dependent code is rescheduled with actual upstream result or task can aggregate; rejected duplicate work does not repeatedly collide. - Boundary reachability: the shipped graph uses GraphState and specialistpayload for every concurrent Send; the code dependency guard runs inside the standard coordinator path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: this is more than generic routing because the source names concrete simultaneous-write failures and stale same-hop inputs, and implements attenuation with feedback; the decisive collision policy is first-party deterministic constructor machinery, not agent-selected negotiation. - Distinct S1 units: parallel math/physics, chemistry, reasoning, code, research, RAG and visualization specialists in a requested fan-out. - Inter-S1 disturbance: simultaneous writes to status/intermediateresults and dependent code lacking same-hop math/chemistry work product. - Attenuating coordination relation: reducer-backed per-specialist state merger, restricted Send payload and one-shot staged code dispatch on prerequisite availability. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: merged intermediate state determines the next coordinator route; the staged second-hop code agent consumes prior solver outputs. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: reducers and staged dispatch specifically prevent demonstrated parallel-write collisions and stale-prerequisite calculations, rather than merely forwarding tasks. - Evidence: reducer witness, minimal Send and stage routing, dependency-staging guard. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high for collision/staging path; no measured multi-agent contention trace. - Caveats: last-wins scalar merging does not solve arbitrary semantic disagreement or concurrent external tool conflicts; no autonomous shared-resource negotiation is demonstrated.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no separately established whole-system current resource/commitment regulation at this one-query graph boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: work cells may consume tokens, fail tools or repeatedly revise, but response is bounded by developer-set hops and fixed model/provider choices. - Decisive decision or feedback right: coordinator picks task specialists and critic-driven retries, while fixed hop caps can terminate; neither supplies evidenced shared resource bargaining, whole-current commitment control or accountability interventions across independent operations. - Decision owner: model for task decomposition and developer/operator for static limits; no distinct first-party whole-system S3 authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: MAXAGENTHOPS, provider fallback, step flags, graph termination and API status/streaming. - Closure path: current-task results and critic suggestions return to the same task loop, not to a whole-system resource-priority controller with return to multiple S1 commitments. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: naming the entry node &quot;coordinator&quot; and providing hard limits is insufficient to establish S3; its view is of the one task, not a managed population of current commitments. - Evidence: coordinator routing, graph, settings. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high for shipped request boundary. - Caveats: external API deployment schedulers are not first-party S3 owners in this assessment.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge a bounded class of specialist solution claims through a separate checking path before answer publication. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incorrect algebra roots, unit/dimension mismatch, numeric/symbolic disagreement, chemistry mass imbalance and conflicting model-specialist accounts. - Decisive decision or feedback right: a separate critic model evaluates original query, intermediate results, raw reasoning trace and programmatic rechecks, issues PASS/REVISE/FAIL and suggestions; failed independent programmatic checks override any critic PASS. - Decision owner: first-party wired critic agent for semantic audit judgment, with deterministic independent re-derivation and hard failure override; the solver specialists do not choose the critic&#39;s verdict. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SymPy re-solving of original recorded equation, numeric cross-check, Pint dimensions, chemistry mass-balance routines, budgeted critic payload, verdict parser and graph gate. - Closure path: specialist claims/raw trace → independent re-derivation and critic judgment → criticreport → routefromcritic reopens specific specialist work (unless capped), or PASS allows aggregation; unresolved findings are surfaced in final answer. - Boundary reachability: buildgraph.py mandates criticnode before aggregation in the full graph; criticagent.py is a shipped node, not the optional offline evalharness. Fast school and /chat-doc bypasses are excluded from this positive mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a distinct model call judges cross-agent consistency and mistakes instead of merely accepting their output; separate deterministic algorithms rederive selected claims from lower-level evidence. Static override enforces the check but does not choose the critic&#39;s full audit findings. - Claim being audited: math/physics/chemistry correctness and consistency of specialist-generated answer components (not general correctness of arbitrary website facts or external provider output). - Ordinary reporting path: specialists write intermediateresults and summarized reasoning/answer paths for the aggregator. - Complementary access path: critic receives original query, raw per-specialist trace/tool inputs and a separately recalculated SymPy/numeric/unit/chemistry check, rather than only the aggregator&#39;s success assertion. - Independence boundary: distinct critic node/model invocation plus programmatic re-derivation independent of solver-produced numeric answers; both remain within one local graph/provider environment and trace inputs can still share upstream modeling errors. - Who acts on findings: the graph routes REVISE/FAIL into the coordinator&#39;s bounded rerun, and the aggregator reports unresolved faults at the hop limit. - Evidence: critic audit and verdict override, fresh algebra recheck and unit checks, returned critic control edge, unresolved handling. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium, restricted to corroborated classes of mathematical/structural claims; not observed audit effectiveness. - Caveats: a routine critic name alone would not qualify; the positive mapping depends on complementary raw-trace access and independent recomputation. The fallback may PASS when checks are unavailable, no independent external web-ground-truth audit exists, and no provider-backed run was executed.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no supported prospective environmental-model and capability-adaptation loop beyond present query solving. - Disturbance / variety regulated: research results, changing APIs and remembered user context can alter a current answer, but are not developed into future operating strategies. - Decisive decision or feedback right: research Tavily, PDF RAG and FAISS/Qdrant memory supply current-task evidence; developer/operator configures providers, model lists and future code. - Decision owner: task agents choose retrieval for the present problem, while maintainers/operators own durable capability changes outside the assessed graph. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Tavily, RAG retrieval, saved turns, provider fallback, offline eval cases, static model/configuration. - Closure path: new facts or recalled turns feed the next answer, with no distinct prospective option selection returning to changed harness capability or S3. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: web search and persistence alone are S1 information gathering, not outside-and-then organizational adaptation. - Evidence: research node, memory façade, RAG. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high at shipped service boundary. - Caveats: model providers can evolve externally, but that evolution is not a first-party AXIOM S4 decision owner.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no legitimate first-party ultimate identity/policy decision-and-return path in the shipped service. - Disturbance / variety regulated: credentials, code safety, allowed execution and API access are governed by operator/developer settings, not a runtime policy authority balancing identity decisions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: operator sets AXIOMAPIKEY, provider keys, hop/code limits; static blocklist/middleware enforces these inputs and agents solve within them. - Decision owner: external API deployer/developer for security and task constraints, not a first-party S5 autonomous or closed parent mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: API key middleware, MAXAGENTHOPS, static code blocklist, subprocess timeout, config/default prompts. - Closure path: startup rules gate requests/tools, without identity-level proposal → legitimate policy authority → authoritative decision → return governing subsequent operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: technical authorization and per-task acceptance/retry do not confer ultimate-policy authority. - Evidence: settings, API middleware, guardrails. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high for the shipped first-party service. - Caveats: public repository contribution decisions and operator-local admin choices are adjacent human governance, not in-run S5 closure.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/fb4819be4a410725dfe874e8613b665b53d7eee5/assessments/kh/khanuzeb-axiom.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit fb4819be4a410725dfe874e8613b665b53d7eee5\">fb4819b &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-17T20:54:30+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/hamish-mackie/sloop\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Sloop</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-17\">2026-11-17</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/hamish-mackie/sloop/tree/0d86a9193e80e9527f6ad3b09904030d6a2877ed\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 0d86a9193e80e9527f6ad3b09904030d6a2877ed\">0d86a91 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Execute model-authored coding changes requested by a committed ticket, with scoped tools and feedback from the run worktree. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Source changes, ticket requirements, failed commands and task ambiguity require the running agent to decide edits and tools. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose how to change the code and what to commit within the dispatched ticket; the scheduler does not plan the change. - Decision owner: The model-backed worker subprocess launched for the ticket via the supported external agent CLI target (Claude Code, Codex or OpenCode). - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: First-party ticket admission, worktree creation, prompt/agent driver, durable run and evidence log, process supervision and commit check. - Closure path: Agent receives its ticket and command observations, edits and commits in its worktree; Sloop observes Git evidence and advances or returns work based on flow verdicts. - Boundary reachability: The shipped init config exposes working provider targets and the default flow has an actual agent stage; the daemon launches the selected provider in a first-party supervised run. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The provider-backed coding agent chooses implementation details; Rust gate decisions enforce process and Git conditions but cannot independently write task-specific code. - Evidence: README; operating concepts; default flow; run driver; worker execution. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: Limit interference among multiple concurrently dispatched coding units competing for project resources and integration of a changing shared branch. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unbounded concurrent workers can oversubscribe process capacity, duplicate one ticket claim, or race against branch changes before merge. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select only eligible trigger-backed tickets for currently available capacity; refuse duplicate claims, and force re-sync/re-verify of a branch after an integration race. - Decision owner: Deterministic dispatcher and configured flow gates own the immediate coordination policy. An autonomous owner of changing inter-worker priorities is not supplied. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: One admission dispatcher, conditional lease, max-parallel gate, dependency checks, isolated run branches and ff-only merge train. - Closure path: Blocked tickets remain unspawned and become eligible after a dependency merges or a slot opens; stale sync/merge races cause the flow to re-enter sync and verify rather than silently integrate stale work. - Boundary reachability: These checks run in the standard daemon and packaged flows with no plugin or invented external coordinator required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The code applies configured coordination rules. A developer would need to compose an autonomous agent that can decide broader inter-worker adjustments, so the evidenced function-specific constructor is C rather than A. - Distinct S1 units: Separate agent subprocesses with their own tickets, model context and Git worktrees. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Contending for the same daemon run slots and common project branch integration while dependencies may be unsatisfied. - Attenuating coordination relation: A single capacity/claim gate and source-aware sync/ff-only integration controls suppress duplicated or unsafe concurrent work. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Released capacity and blocker completion trigger later work; a failed fast-forward makes the worker flow re-sync and reverify before another merge. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The specific interference being attenuated is double admission and stale integration of simultaneously active workers, evidenced by lease and merge-race rules rather than messaging alone. - Evidence: operating concepts (dispatcher/claim/merge); dispatcher; eligibility; merge train; flow replay. - Basis: structural -\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: Allow the local project operator to regulate the presently running and pending job portfolio. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Work can stall, overrun allocated hours, fail validation, or no longer match the operator&#39;s current priorities. - Decisive decision or feedback right: View current project tickets and runs; choose to hold, release, cancel, retry, pause/resume dispatch or queue selected work. - Decision owner: The operator of the local Sloop installation in the parent control mode. The daemon enforces configured scheduler rules, but does not autonomously choose substantive priorities. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Project show/status, output and stage evidence, authenticated operator socket, run cancellation and hold/ready state transitions. - Closure path: An operator&#39;s action changes durable ticket/daemon state and subsequent dispatch eligibility or live run disposition. - Boundary reachability: The operator CLI/socket and show/hold/ready/pause/cancel commands are first-party shipped controls. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The decision to hold or release task work in response to the whole portfolio belongs to the operator; rate limits and triggers are deterministic supporting machinery. - Whole-system current view: Sloop show exposes project tickets, active runs, blocked/dependent tickets and stated scheduling reasons across the one local project. - Current-control decision scope: Current admission, cancellation, pause/resume and task commitments for the local work portfolio. - Evidence: operating concepts; CLI; eligibility; dispatcher. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: Operator control is limited to the local running project. A scheduled worker&#39;s routine retries or a single narrow override alone are not the parent S3 evidence.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Obtain separately produced evidence-based review of an implementer&#39;s completion claim before integration. - Disturbance / variety regulated: An agent can produce a successful exit or commits with an incorrect or incomplete change. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A separate reviewer agent directly inspects the diff and task, runs relevant tests and issues a reported pass/fail judgment; the deterministic flow accepts/rejects according to that report. - Decision owner: A fresh review-stage model subprocess, distinct from the implementation-stage worker and given a separate review prompt. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Readonly reviewer tool restriction (Bash rather than Write/Edit), review-stage socket token, mandatory reported verdict, append-only stage evidence and returnto build on failure. - Closure path: A reported failure re-enters build with a bounded retry; no verdict is itself a failure, and integration follows only after review and further required checks pass. - Boundary reachability: The init-supplied default flow includes review with a separate Claude execution stage and concrete review prompt; an optional train flow deliberately omits review. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The reviewer chooses its substantive adequacy conclusion from the diff, task and tests; the flow&#39;s code only enforces and records that decision. - Claim being audited: A build-stage agent&#39;s assertion that committed code meets the posted ticket. - Ordinary reporting path: Build agent transcript, exit code and new-commit evidence. - Complementary access path: Independent review stage reads current Git diff against default branch, task brief and tests rather than only the worker&#39;s final message. - Independence boundary: A second subprocess/prompt and output channel with limited tools; a deployment can still use the same provider/model and no external human review is implied. - Who acts on findings: The first-party flow returns a negative verdict to build for correction; passing evidence is prerequisite to downstream sync/verify/merge. - Evidence: default flow; review prompt; flow replay; operating concepts; review panel (optional reviewer quorum). - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No shipped function-specific agent path was found to observe external futures, generate prospective adaptation alternatives and return a selected option into future system capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing providers, user needs and integration constraints would require prospective redesign beyond one ticket. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No supported actor owns a future-oriented organizational adaptation judgment inside the daemon. - Decision owner: Not established at the reviewed runtime boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Fixed retries/cooldowns, status histories, editable flows and configured provider targets support current operation but do not select adaptive policy. - Closure path: Not established for a prospective S4 decision; operator-authored changes are outside the automatic run scope. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The project expressly presents itself as a deterministic scheduling kernel without planning or memory; observable cooldowns are short-term operational recovery, not a prospective capability-learning loop. - Evidence: operating concepts; configuration; dispatcher; flow replay. - Basis: explicit + structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Function: Set the standing project workflow and acceptance policy that defines which jobs Sloop may run and which outcomes may be integrated. - Disturbance / variety regulated: The local owner&#39;s evolving expectations about provider authority, quality gates and execution windows must continue to constrain future automatic runs. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select and author durable project config/flows: targets, allowed hours/capacity, test command, reviewer requirement, retry policy and merge conditions. - Decision owner: The legitimate repository/project operator editing committed policy/configuration before dispatch. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Versioned YAML flow/config files, flow parsing/validation, per-run flow snapshot, immutable stage/evidence replay, daemon policy gates. - Closure path: Owner commits/loads the standing policy; daemon validates the flow and records a snapshot at run admission; later agent work, reviewer stage and merges obey the selected policy. - Boundary reachability: The installed init scaffolds config and default flow files; CLI posting and daemon startup validate/apply this owner-authored policy. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A worker chooses edits but cannot redefine the authorized task/merge policy in its existing admitted flow; the policy authority remains with the local operator. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: What is acceptable autonomous coding activity and which evidence is required before Sloop calls project work complete. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: Local project owner/operator, not the provider subprocess or OpenSiro reviewer. - Return-to-operation path: Persisted config/flow → validation and admitted flow snapshot → enforced run stages, acceptance verdicts and subsequent dispatch. - Evidence: README; configuration; CLI; default flow; flow replay. - Basis: structural -\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/c306d45c0030ce130501bbed82f8ccc44897caff/assessments/sl/sloop.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit c306d45c0030ce130501bbed82f8ccc44897caff\">c306d45 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-12T16:00:11+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/xai-org/grok-build\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Grok Build</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-12\">2026-10-12</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/xai-org/grok-build/tree/4247f661689354b831191f11eeeac8424993fe3d\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 4247f661689354b831191f11eeeac8424993fe3d\">4247f66 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Model-guided terminal coding actions against a real file/shell/VCS workspace, with live tool-result feedback and continued model turns. - Disturbance / variety regulated: User requirements, changing code/build/test state, tool errors and provider response variability. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose and execute task-specific files, shell and code actions and adapt to tool results. - Decision owner: Hosted Grok Build root coding agent with model-guided tool call decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Rust xai-grok-shell agent runtime, model sampler, xai-grok-tools, VCS/workspace interfaces, permission and stop policies. - Closure path: User coding goal → model selects tool/action → tool effect → new observations → model continuation/finish. - Boundary reachability: Official terminal/headless/ACP grok modes activate the model/task/tool Rust agent from the packaged root shell and connect to first-party workspace tool operations. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The first-party root uses actual task-conditioned model/tool execution; deterministic policy and tool runner restrict, but do not replace, the choice of coding actions. - Evidence: crates/codegen/xai-grok-shell/src/agent/mvpagent/mod.rs; crates/codegen/xai-grok-tools/src/implementations/grokbuild/task/mod.rs; README.md - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: ? - Function: Candidate coordination of concurrently operating subagent S1 units; no proven specific cross-child disturbance arbitration beyond quotas/queue and opt-in checkout separation. - Distinct S1 units: Independent child coding-agent tasks with separate subagent identifiers and optional worktrees. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Possible shared-workspace concurrent edits/collisions; source offers isolation but not a proven content-specific arbitration trigger. - Attenuating coordination relation: Bounded coordinator admission and optional worktree isolation are available, but not demonstrated as detecting a particular inter-S1 disturbance and returning corrective coordination. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Child completion/query and queue release inform the parent; a specific cross-child interference remedy is unproven. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: S2 not credited; the observed controls could be generic concurrency limits, session lifecycle and optional separation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Possible overlapping child changes to a shared workspace, exhaustion of concurrency slots, stale child messages. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Subagent spawn admission is bounded, and optional isolation=worktree alters child workspace; no definite interference-dependent S2 owner/feedback path located. - Decision owner: Coordinator actor and parent/root choice; function-specific S2 ownership unresolved. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Single-writer coordinator, Admission concurrency ceiling, pending/active queues, worktree option and separate child lifecycle. - Closure path: Child events pass through queued/active lifecycle, but no demonstrated inter-child content-conflict sensor and returned remedial assignment has been established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Concurrency caps, independent child actors and worktree isolation alone are not evidence of a cross-S1, conflict-specific regulation loop. - Evidence: crates/codegen/xai-grok-tools/src/implementations/grokbuild/task/admission.rs; crates/codegen/xai-grok-tools/src/implementations/grokbuild/task/coordinator.rs; crates/codegen/xai-grok-tools/src/implementations/grokbuild/task/mod.rs - Basis: unknown -\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: Root coding agent controls current child task commitments, interrupts and collects returned subordinate results in its own team recursion. - Whole-system current view: Parent/root has child task list, pending/active/completed state and returned outputs in its own session&#39;s bounded team. - Current-control decision scope: Child task commitments, start/stop, future instructions and result integration within one coding-team run. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Running, queued or stalled subagent tasks, completion/failure and changes to parent coding goal. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Model-root may invoke task spawn, inspect running children, await child outputs, send child messages and cancel/redirect child tasks. - Decision owner: Model-driven Grok Build parent/root agent using packaged task and subagent lifecycle tools; coordinator implements these decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Task tool, coordinator registry, list/query/kill controls, active message delivery, root/child ownership enforcement. - Closure path: Child progress and completion → coordinator result/query to parent → root model selects further task/message/cancel or integrates child output → changed child activity. - Boundary reachability: First-party Task/subagent query/cancel/message interfaces are wired to the parent agent and coordinator and admit actual independent worker attempts rather than merely showing a static status dashboard. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model root chooses consequential team task commitments based on child outcomes, not merely reading one worker&#39;s progress bar. - Evidence: crates/codegen/xai-grok-shell/src/extensions/task.rs; crates/codegen/xai-grok-shell/src/agent/subagent/spawn.rs; crates/codegen/xai-grok-tools/src/implementations/grokbuild/task/coordinator/query.rs; crates/codegen/xai-grok-tools/src/implementations/grokbuild/task/coordinator.rs - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: ? - Function: No established independently sourced complementary audit of the producing coding agents or team current-operation claims. - Claim being audited: That the child or root-produced code actually fulfills its requested task. - Ordinary reporting path: A completed child returns a result into the parent Task/output and coordinator message path. - Complementary access path: Unresolved; neither cloud inline review comment records nor an optional reviewer persona prove separately observed source/evidence. - Independence boundary: A separately executing auditor with direct artifact sampling and corrective feedback is not established at the declared mode. - Who acts on findings: Parent may act on child output/messages, but no guaranteed complementary audit finding route was identified. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Erroneous developer work, unverified result claims and hidden defects. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A separate reviewer persona/agent can be requested as a child, but guaranteed independent direct-source observational audit and corrective return are not established as a shipped built-in closure. - Decision owner: Unresolved; review-comment extension stores UI events and is not an independent auditing agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Subagent task/persona facilities; ACP review extension; standard tool feedback. - Closure path: Review comments/child messages may be consumed by root, but source-to-independent-reviewer audit → corrective control path is unproven. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A review UI event or model-provided reviewer persona is insufficient without materially independent observation and change in current work. - Evidence: crates/codegen/xai-grok-shell/src/extensions/review.rs; crates/codegen/xai-grok-tools/src/implementations/grokbuild/task/coordinator.rs; crates/codegen/xai-grok-tools/src/implementations/grokbuild/task/backendtests.rs - Basis: unknown -\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: ? - Function: Memory, task planning and persistent/long-running sessions are present, but no complete outside-and-then foresight and adaptation closure is established. - External distinction: Workspace and model inputs may refer to external trends or code, without an identified sensing-and-foresight role. - Future / prospective distinction: Persisted memory and long-running tasks affect later operations, but future time alone is not a prospective decision. - Adaptation option generated: Not established as a future organizational capability redesign choice. - Path back into current capability / S3: Memory/session resume can influence subsequent coding decisions; strategic capability-change closure remains unresolved. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changed future user coding needs, project architecture and external developments. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Model can adapt a present coding plan and maintain memory, but capacity-changing future-oriented decision ownership is unresolved. - Decision owner: Unresolved between user/configuration and model decisions on immediate tasks. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: xai-grok-memory, session resume, task tools and configuration. - Closure path: Saved context returns to future model tasks, without an established external prospective option/apply loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Persistent memory or near-term task planning is not automatically VSM S4. - Evidence: crates/codegen/xai-grok-memory/src/lib.rs; crates/codegen/xai-grok-shell/src/agent/mvpagent/mod.rs; crates/codegen/xai-grok-shell/src/extensions/task.rs - Basis: unknown -\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: ? - Function: Product identity, permissions and policy constrain operations but do not yet establish a function-specific ultimate-policy closure. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: What Grok Build root/team is ultimately allowed to do and for whom. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: No confirmed ultimate-policy actor at this recursion; session credentials and user permission gates exist but do not qualify by themselves. - Return-to-operation path: Sandbox and permission decisions filter tool actions, without a demonstrated complete governing policy feedback path. - Disturbance / variety regulated: User trust, sandbox boundaries, resource access and model policy. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Runtime and human user authorize particular actions; no definitive highest-policy actor and feedback reorganization demonstrated. - Decision owner: Unresolved at project coding-team recursion; user permission enforcement is not enough to credit S5=P. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Sandbox restrictions, workspace permissions, client authentication and configuration. - Closure path: Allowed/denied actions change execution, but an ultimate identity/policy choice returned across organizational operation is not established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Enforcing sandbox or workspace permissions is narrower than ultimate organizational policy authority. - Evidence: crates/codegen/xai-grok-sandbox/src/lib.rs; crates/codegen/xai-grok-shell/src/agent/mvpagent/mod.rs; crates/codegen/xai-grok-tools/src/implementations/grokbuild/task/rootcontrol.rs - Basis: unknown -\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/e5406871ace31d3eb4a8b9394d2b0f5deebbafc0/assessments/grok-build.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit e5406871ace31d3eb4a8b9394d2b0f5deebbafc0\">e540687 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-17T19:26:33+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/kstawiski/evidence-gated-scientific-agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Evidence Bench</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-17\">2026-10-17</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/kstawiski/evidence-gated-scientific-agent/tree/85b396a824dfad6ec0880cbe07d86d3e24920ae8\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 85b396a824dfad6ec0880cbe07d86d3e24920ae8\">85b396a &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Perform research, produce a plan/report and generate grounded scientific evidence through actual model-directed source/tool interaction. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Uncertain scientific question, varying source data and code results, inconsistent literature, missing provenance and model hallucinations. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Qwen/Gemma planning models develop concrete alternative scientific plans, synthesizer selects a MasterPlan; first-party model researcher and reporter take bounded retrieval/computation/tool steps to produce a scientific report. Repair models use new observed findings to generate next revised outputs. - Decision owner: First-party ADK/model role logic invoked by runscientifictask; external inference is a configured backend, not an outside authored research operator. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Plan proposal/research/report prompts, ADK workflow, scientific ToolPolicy, retrieval evidence ledger, typed computation tools and deterministic lints. - Closure path: objective/inputs → independent model plan candidates and auditable master → model/tool research and computation observations → model report with cited outputs → critique feedback → model repair/new report. - Boundary reachability: The shipped orchestrator&#39;s runscientifictask calls buildplanningworkflow / runtyped, then producereport, which creates configured first-party ADK research/report toolsets. The same entry is reached through exported run and services. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Model planners/researchers select substantive plan/evidence and responsive report/repair content from observed sources and tool results; static control and policy guardrails are separate. - Evidence: scientificagent/workflow.py; scientificagent/orchestrator.py; scientificagent/runtime.py; scientificagent/execution.py. - Basis: Reachable source-level model/tool/evidence-feedback implementation. - Confidence: High on installed loops, not measured scientific correctness or model inference. - Caveats: Actual Qwen/Gemma backend and any enabled optional code or MCP tools must be configured; benchmarking claims not imported.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No demonstrated control attenuating a material inter-S1 operational collision rather than merely collecting parallel proposals and sequencing task stages. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Two planners may provide different suggestions, but they have no shared mutable working target or independently competing active operational commitment requiring stabilization. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Qwen synthesizes proposals into one plan; deterministic serial stage/gates select what comes next. Neither path detects and resolves interference among independent ongoing S1 operations. - Decision owner: Planning join/synthesizer and single-run controller. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ADK maxconcurrency=2 parallel planner generation, JoinNode, typed candidate collection, and final deterministic lint. - Closure path: Two plan alternatives → synthesis → master plan, which is task content formation. There is no distinct operating-unit clash → anti-interference choice → changed sibling activity feedback documented. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A comparison of concurrently generated plans, their disagreement and synthesis is not by itself a Beer S2 loop, nor do research budgets alone prove inter-S1 mutual correction. - Evidence: scientificagent/workflow.py; scientificagent/orchestrator.py; scientificagent/policy.py. - Basis: Bounded negative assessment of candidate planning/phase-control mechanisms. - Confidence: Medium: deliberate independent planning and synthesis is source-real, but no specific inter-S1 disturbance/attenuation contract was found. - Caveats: A deployment with multiple simultaneous runs may add coordination in a separate server layer; not credited without source proof for the assessed run.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: C - Function: Whole-current scientific run control: when plans are accepted, which research/evidence/critique phase may proceed, how scarce model/tool/repair attempts are budgeted and when final outputs are supported or withheld. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unreliable plans, unsupported evidence, unbounded tool/model calls, failing audit findings, code/tool nonprogress and bounded available repair resources. - Decisive decision or feedback right: runscientifictask orchestrates planning/admissibility, reassigns rejected plans to repair, conditionally admits research, enforces research budgets, orders deterministic validation and independent reviewer, initiates limited repair, and sets final status rather than promoting rejected reports. - Decision owner: First-party deterministic Python controller and configured ResearchBudgetController, not autonomous scientific principal or another model owning organization-wide allocation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Planning.status, MaxRepairRounds, ToolPolicy, sandbox restrictions, deterministic lints, evidence/provenance ledger, needsrepair and finalrunstatus. - Closure path: Current plan/evidence/critic state and resource use → controller selects stop, execute next unit or bounded repair → new operations/results → updated audit+status → repeat or present final result/needs-human with recorded reasons. - Whole-system current view: Active master plan, model research state, evidence/retrieval and computation, deterministic validity, separate audit report, cumulative tool counts and attempts across one full scientific run. - Current-control decision scope: The constructor controls operational commitments of specialized planning, research/reporting, critique and revision operations, their admissibility and their shared run budget. - Boundary reachability: runscientifictask allocates ResearchBudgetController, executes prepareandauditplan, checks planning.status before producereport, validates outputs, invokes independent report review and loops repairs while needsrepair and the configured round cap hold; finalrunstatus controls final admission. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Decisive phase/budget/stop choices are expressed by static controller rules rather than a free model explicitly managing whole-current resource allocation; S3=C, not S3=A. - Evidence: scientificagent/orchestrator.py; scientificagent/workflow.py; scientificagent/policy.py; scientificagent/config.py. - Basis: Source-attested current-commitment controller with feedback from multiple first-party operating roles. - Confidence: High for constructor current-run regulation; no external scientific-team budget governance claimed. - Caveats: Single-run scope is not project portfolio governance, and some phases use the same model provider for different prompts.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Independently challenge active research plan and report claims from an alternative model/evidence path, with critic findings feeding real repair or an unresolved stop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unsound scientific plans, citations without source support, fabricated computation values, contradictory data/display, incorrect visual readings and unsupported final conclusions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A separately instructed Gemma PLANAUDITOR audits the Qwen-generated MasterPlan; independent REPORTAUDITOR evaluates claimed results against retrieved/computed artifact evidence and deterministic validation, while visual review can inspect actual figure/table pixel inputs. Critic verdict/blocking findings can force a repair or prevent supported terminal status. - Decision owner: First-party distinct model-based Gemma critic invocation and structured VerificationReport judgment; constructor separately validates/reconciles the critic&#39;s outputs and commits control transitions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Plan audit packet with independently generated checklist, retrieval/computation evidence, detached visual bytes, deterministic validators, unique audit artifacts and bounded repair envelopes. - Closure path: Qwen plan/report claim → independent Gemma plan/report/artifact review → structured blocking/nonblocking findings → controller&#39;s planningstatus or needsrepair chooses repair/withholding → revised plan/report and re-audit → supported/needs-human termination. - Claim being audited: Whether the plan&#39;s scientific method is admissible and whether report conclusions/displays are justified by gathered evidence. - Ordinary reporting path: Qwen research/reporter structured scientific report, planned steps and source/output claims. - Complementary access path: Gemma uses separately instructed audit prompts and controller-provided source/compute/citation/validation evidence plus actual rasterized displays, rather than merely accepting report narrative or self-score. - Independence boundary: Distinct model-critic role and alternative evidence/report read path; not a different OS trust domain, protected human team or globally independent provider. Strictly bounded to enabled first-party run configuration. - Who acts on findings: Constructor prepareandauditplan and report repair loop consume Gemma&#39;s verdict and findings; repairplan/ producereport receive them, final finalrunstatus refuses supported unless independent review is passing. - Boundary reachability: buildplanningworkflow invokes auditmasterplan on the actual master, and runscientifictask invokes auditreportresilient on the actual report/retrieval/computation. planningstatus, needsrepair and final status consume those outcomes to change installed execution, not merely save benchmark scores. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The alternative critical reading and structured falsification findings are selected by a model with separate audit instructions and evidence packet; deterministic controller owns repair scheduling/format admission but not the semantic critic judgment. S3=A, not just C. - Evidence: scientificagent/workflow.py; scientificagent/orchestrator.py; scientificagent/prompts.py; scientificagent/linting.py. - Basis: Source-reachable independent critic alternate evidence with actual correction-return and bounded blocking. - Confidence: High for model-based audit and repair wiring, medium for effectiveness of critic independence and coverage. - Caveats: On model outage or exhaustion the controller can return inconclusive/needs-human rather than supported. Model critic has its own error modes and deterministic checks sometimes reject contradictory critic instructions.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No established prospective external strategic adaptation of the scientific organization&#39;s own future capability returned into whole-current S3. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Scientific literature discoveries, new input datasets and computational results are changing task environment, but are handled as evidence for the present user research question. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The agents retrieve literature and propose revised scientific methods, yet no first-party function evaluates externally changing opportunities or future organizational capabilities and installs a choice back into ongoing scientific-agent governance. - Decision owner: Model research/plan production and constructor task validation, not a separate future-capability adaptation function. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Literature acquisition, knowledge library, revision tasks, plan synthesis/repair, model and tool configuration. - Closure path: External paper/data → current study evidence/plan → accepted report or within-task repair. No organizational environment foresight → comparative capability options → enduring adaptation choice → current S3 return. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Scientific research may explore future hypotheses, but that is S1 work for a requested scientific study, not automatic evidence of this harness changing its own future operational repertoire. - Evidence: scientificagent/literature.py; scientificagent/workflow.py; scientificagent/orchestrator.py; scientificagent/knowledge.py. - Basis: Bounded negative review of strongest apparent prospective literature and revision flows. - Confidence: Medium-high within one-run recursion. - Caveats: External researchers/operators can improve prompts/tools in later repository revisions; those human activities are outside the reviewed deployed agent organization.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No owned ultimate policy/identity/ethos settlement path distinguished from fixed scientific integrity and tool safety rules. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Scientific risk labels, provenance obligations, tool permissions, domain/language constraints, evaluation failure and human reconsideration. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Task requester and first-party configured controller impose a risk-sensitive scientific protocol and locked task constraints; on exhausted attempts the controller reports requireshumandecision, but it does not adjudicate ultimate organization identity/policy conflicts. - Decision owner: Human scientific owner outside runtime for ultimate risk and approval; fixed first-party admission rules for current-run integrity. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: MasterPlan/task protocol binding, ToolPolicy, static scientific safety rules, sandbox defaults, provenance manifest and risk classifications. - Closure path: Inputs/risk constraints → fixed scientific gate and possible human escalation. No first-party policy dispute → ultimate legitimate purpose/ethos decision → binding return to organizational governance. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Fail-closed status, provenance hashes, immutable task locks and user escalation are robust current-run controls, not a system deciding its ultimate goals. - Evidence: scientificagent/workflow.py; scientificagent/orchestrator.py; scientificagent/policy.py; docs/MVPSCOPE.md. - Basis: Bounded negative review of policy/risk/identity surfaces. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: A human scientific review board may have ultimate policy authority outside this product; no inherited S5 parent relation is scored without a first-party exposed closing loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/066ea03ff0b6e663b615313395c5ba10bf1c9f8a/assessments/evidence-bench.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 066ea03ff0b6e663b615313395c5ba10bf1c9f8a\">066ea03 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-13T22:09:26+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/whycorporation/whycodes\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">WhyCodes</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-13\">2026-10-13</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/whycorporation/whycodes/tree/ef5126824739673d93815b29456ba3e5bdddf87c\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision ef5126824739673d93815b29456ba3e5bdddf87c\">ef51268 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: take discretionary local codebase engineering actions and repairs via first-party model-selected tools, and independently execute model-owned delegated subtask work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unknown code state, compilation/test failures, incomplete edits, missing information and live tool error observations. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the root or child coding model chooses its own repository read/edit/shell operations and responds to real outcomes. - Decision owner: active language-model agent within the standard runtime; a spawned general worker has its own model/tool context. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: turn loop, tool/permission gate, local session and persistent history, code tools and optional subagent process/worktree. - Closure path: engineering request → model-selected tools → actual local execution/result → next tool/repair choice or completion. - Boundary reachability: normal whycodes agent loop and first-party registered task/swarm, not only tests. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: fixed dispatch/sandbox and memory cannot choose a context-sensitive code change without the model&#39;s discretionary action. - Evidence: README.md; turn.rs; gate.rs; dispatch.rs; subagent.rs; mod.rs - Basis: explicit / structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Distinct S1 units: concurrent first-party model-running general or other delegated coding workers in one swarm task wave; each has independent model turn/tool decisions and an assigned scoped goal. - Inter-S1 disturbance: two worker S1s can claim/write an overlapping source file in the same checkout, observe an out-of-date version of another worker&#39;s changed file, or contribute overlapping changes that would overwrite diverged main checkout content when merging isolated worktrees. - Attenuating coordination relation: FileClaimRegistry refuses double ownership, logs/reports stale reads and actively emits FileConflict; preclaims reject inconsistent paths before work; default separate worktrees quarantine writes and three-way guarded merge declines conflicting paths rather than silently clobbering main. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: a conflicting same-checkout writer receives a tool denial/conflict owner, an attempted preclaim returns an immediate error to parent, and worktree conflicts appear in worker/aggregate results so the parent can narrow scopes or retry/reassign. Nonconflicting file changes are applied to the main checkout. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: these first-party checks regulate concrete multi-S1 interference over the same file&#39;s ownership or merged effects, not merely launch tasks or display messages. - Function: attenuate source-file/merge interference among multiple independently model-running coding S1 workers. - Disturbance / variety regulated: concurrent overlapping file writes, stale reads and conflicting post-worktree integration. - Decisive decision or feedback right: deterministic same-checkout file-claim gate and three-way merge decide whether a conflicting write or integration may proceed; conflict event/result names the current owner/path. - Decision owner: deterministic first-party coordination constructor (C), not an autonomous S2 model. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: shared registry, worker-specific context, read/write tool hooks, preclaim checks, worktree creation/merge, conflict events and status reports. - Closure path: independent workers approach same source path → exclusive claim or guarded merge compares ownership/base/main → unsafe action is blocked or marked conflicted → affected worker or root receives conflict information → subsequent work can adopt safe scoped action/repair. - Boundary reachability: types.rs default enables swarm/worktrees; gate.rs and dispatch.rs execute code-owned swarm entrypoint and claims/merge in supported modes. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: interference-specific admission/merge outcome is coded and remains if model decision-making is removed; the model may respond as S3 but does not own the fixed S2 conflict rule. - Evidence: swarm.rs; fileclaims.rs; swarmworktree.rs; dispatch.rs; subagent.rs; swarmhub.rs; modtests.rs - Basis: explicit first-party code paths and regression tests - Confidence: high for bounded technical write/merge conflict regulation - Caveats: same-checkout conflicts are observed only through claimed file operations; undeclared semantic conflicts and independent manually launched sessions are not guaranteed covered. Worktree conflicts can remain for manual/follow-on resolution.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Whole-system current view: the root&#39;s swarm result aggregates every invoked worker&#39;s type, goal, completion/failure, duration, output, merge conflicts and applied file effects into one current-wave report; the parent retains the ongoing model conversation and tool outcomes. - Current-control decision scope: root model selects an initial set of worker roles, scope/path ownership and concurrency, then can elect an additional/corrective wave, change active task scope assignments for the next wave, or directly repair integration issues based on aggregate current worker status/conflicts. - Function: manage the current set of delegated coding commitments through model-selected work partitioning and evidence-based follow-on assignment/correction between waves. - Disturbance / variety regulated: uneven worker completion, failed tasks, merge conflicts, integration tradeoffs and current coding workload allocation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: root model selects which present work to assign to which subordinate type and scope, and how to revise current remaining work after observing whole-wave results. - Decision owner: top-level model over the supported first-party swarm mode, not fixed semaphore/worktree merge code. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: swarm task spec/tool contract, aggregate report with per-member status/conflicts, model turn tool-result return, and S2 guarded application. - Closure path: model assigns a concurrent worker roster → independent workers act and current report returns including failures/conflicts → model can choose changed worker brief/scope, follow-up task or repair → next first-party tool/run applies the revised commitment. - Boundary reachability: default-enabled first-party swarm in actual Agent::runturn execution, not an unwired utility or example. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic worker scheduling and reporting cannot determine appropriate future current-job assignments or repairs absent the root model&#39;s discretion. - Evidence: swarm.rs; swarm.rs; dispatch.rs; gate.rs; turn.rs; types.rs; subagent.rs - Basis: structural, with supported tool-model response loop - Confidence: medium for wave-boundary model-control closure, no live provider session run - Caveats: manager sees consolidated wave results after workers settle; real-time mid-wave organizational reprioritization/preemption and whole-deployment resource policy are not claimed. This is current multi-worker job management, not prospective S4.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no shipped separately owned complementary reviewer established to independently inspect an implementing S1&#39;s current completion claim and return a corrective verdict to the first-party S3 current manager. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incorrect reported code completion or regressions accepted without a separate direct-evidence challenge. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no independent reviewer verdict/return path within assessed standard modes. A swarm report describes worker outcomes; file merge check detects structural conflicts but not independent semantic quality of coding claims. - Decision owner: implementing coding model and the root can run ordinary tests/read code; optional human/CI review remains outside system. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: worktree guarded merge, file-claim conflict reports, generic read-only scout/explore workers, permission and test tools. - Closure path: results are reported to parent coding model; no first-party dedicated independent reviewer claim check → manager judgment → required corrective operation established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: generic worker read-only capability or worktree merge safety does not become a separate source-first complementary auditor by labeling output an audit. - Evidence: dispatch.rs; swarm.rs; subagent.rs; swarmworktree.rs; README.md; mod.rs - Basis: structural review of shipped roles/wiring -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no future-oriented environmental intelligence and applied organizational capability adaptation owner demonstrated. - Disturbance / variety regulated: future coding environments, model/provider capability shifts and new classes of tooling. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no model or legitimate parent selects organizational future capabilities then returns an implemented change. - Decision owner: user/config/developers set provider/workflow/skills; root handles present coding work and current worker allocation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: memory, session compaction, skill registry, provider routing, SDK/daemon interface, schedule and contextual tools. - Closure path: configuration and memory inform current actions, but future environmental sensing → strategic capability selection → applied new operating capacity is not wired as one ownership cycle. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: adding workers to the current coding task or recalling previous files is S1/S3 present control, not S4 organizational adaptation. - Evidence: README.md; architecture.md; lib.rs; mod.rs; types.rs - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party ultimate organizational mission/identity policy authority with binding corrective return. - Disturbance / variety regulated: choice over organizational purpose/constitutional priorities, beyond acceptance of individual code edits. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no legitimate S5 owner sets/reconciles final mission values and returns binding governing change to subordinate organizational functions. - Decision owner: operator/project owners configure prompts, tool capabilities and permissions; model selects execution within the declared scope. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: permission policies and user approvals, tool/shell sandbox, session roles, inherited worker restrictions. - Closure path: risky tool actions allowed, denied or paused under user/developer rules; neither constitutes an organizational identity-policy choice nor changes the system&#39;s governing purpose. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: per-tool allowed/denied and sandbox boundaries enforce action safety, not ultimate organization policy. - Evidence: types.rs; mod.rs; gate.rs; subagent.rs; README.md - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/20907c5be09105accc3e1e28053abcab39b2f98d/assessments/whycodes.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 20907c5be09105accc3e1e28053abcab39b2f98d\">20907c5 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"2\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-26T19:45:01+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/omergocmen/CrewCtl\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CrewCtl</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>2</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-26\">2026-10-26</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/omergocmen/CrewCtl/tree/834c399cf28a9e83e7e47f81544ed52dfbeb92cc\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 834c399cf28a9e83e7e47f81544ed52dfbeb92cc\">834c399 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Carry out an assigned code or research task through model-directed inspection, editing/tool use, testing and result reporting. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Repository files, build/test feedback, failed tool calls, task errors and unexpected implementation constraints. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The specialist LLM CLI chooses task-specific implementation and verification actions inside the delegated work; it returns a result/error for subsequent rounds. - Decision owner: Configured coding CLI specialist invoked from the first-party runAssignmentWithRecovery / runCli path, under the assignment&#39;s role and limits. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Subprocess adapter catalog, working-directory sandbox settings, timeouts, retry/failover, task store, optional worktrees and verification commands. - Closure path: Operator-selected assignment → specialist model/tool execution against code → output and observable files → stored state.results → new operator/team context. - Boundary reachability: The installed crewctl engine&#39;s normal runTask path invokes the configured agent executable via runAssignments and runAssignmentWithRecovery, using its returned output in the same live task; fake CLI fixtures are not needed to establish the shipped wiring. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model-driven coding specialist selects actions. CrewCtl orchestrates and constrains them; the externally installed CLI itself is a required runtime dependency and is not incorrectly claimed as CrewCtl source code. - Evidence: engine runAssignments, runCli and runTask, supported adapters, executor role. - Basis: explicit first-party operational wiring; no live external model test. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Installed provider/account capability and task success cannot be inferred from source or mocked CLI tests.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: Prevent operational cross-contamination among simultaneous task teams operating on one source repository. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Two independently running task teams can overwrite each other&#39;s files, reuse working state or misattribute status events when they share a directory and process identity. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The first-party task-level mode constructs separate worker engines and assigns each task a distinct Git worktree/branch and correctly tagged event stream; the configuration/user still selects this mode and no autonomous inter-team conflict arbiter is evidenced. - Decision owner: The concrete isolation and queue-claiming primitive is owned by CrewCtl&#39;s supervisor/worker and worktree runtime; no separate S2 agentic decision owner is closed. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: maxConcurrentTasks, per-worker engine slots, task claiming/skip IDs, worktree creation from HEAD, task-indexed messages and event routing. - Closure path: Concurrent queue items → separate task worker and worktree identity → each specialist reads/writes only its team&#39;s checkout → results committed to separate local branches and events routed to the correct task; any higher-level conflict resolution between output branches is not supplied. - Boundary reachability: The shipped supervisor accepts maxConcurrentTasks &gt; 1 and worktree.mode: task through documented config and invokes worktree.create in the live task path; the native concurrency test exercises two simultaneous team runs, separate checkout paths and correctly associated events. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: This is a specific first-party S2 construction and isolation path, not an autonomous agent evaluating conflicts and changing pairwise coordination; hence C, not A. - Distinct S1 units: Two concurrent per-task specialist coding-agent teams, each with its own operator, task state and repository worktree. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Shared-file modifications, workspace contamination, queue double-claim and cross-task event attribution. - Attenuating coordination relation: The supervisor claims tasks once and routes workers into distinct worktrees/branches with per-task event IDs. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Each team&#39;s next CLI run executes in its own checkout and consumes its own task-specific results instead of the other team&#39;s dirty files or events. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The dedicated worktree/multi-worker mode directly attenuates a concrete interference between independent active task teams, although it stops short of automatic merge/conflict arbitration. - Evidence: worktree runtime, engine concurrent worker slots and task checkout, native concurrency test, opt-in mode defaults. - Basis: first-party source and test-defined interference case. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Disabled by default; C is narrower than resource-priority coordination or agentic resolution of conflicting changes across branches. User must enable concurrency and task worktrees.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: Control the active task team against its current objective, evidence, limits and interruptions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Ambiguous task decomposition, failed delegated work, unavailable agents, red verification gates, incomplete delivery and excessive review rounds. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The operator LLM makes structured assignment/dependency decisions and subsequent continue vs complete/replan decisions from returned current-state reports; deterministic guards constrain or occasionally bypass its judgment. - Decision owner: Configured operator-model CLI with CrewCtl&#39;s first-party plan/review prompts and validation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: normalizeAssignments, role-chain completion, dependency runner, failure feedback, progress state, evidence/diff snapshots, verify gate, completion-proof and budget guards. - Closure path: Task and whole-team context → model-selected assignments → execution/reviewer/verifier evidence → operator model&#39;s next decision → new assignments or guarded task completion. - Boundary reachability: Normal runTask invokes invokeOperatorJson on the planning and review phases using operatorPrompt; decisions reach runAssignments and complete in the same task process. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The operator chooses priorities, specialists, remedial work and task completion from team state. The built-in queue, dependency ordering and hard verification constraints execute rather than originate that plan. - Whole-system current view: Current task prompt, project context, enabled team capabilities, prior assignments/results/messages, verification evidence and resource/round status appear in the operator prompts and state. - Current-control decision scope: Select task-work distribution, delegate or repair blocked work, re-plan remaining commitments, decide completion within the configured guardrails. - Evidence: engine operatorPrompt, runTask, runAssignments, runVerifyGate, operator role. - Basis: explicit first-party model decision and closed task loop. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Fast mode intentionally elides some second-stage operator review; no parent modifier is inferred from manual task creation, start/stop or risky-plan approval.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: Challenge implementation reports with direct code/test inspection and return an independent quality finding into task control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: A specialist can report success while files are unchanged, acceptance criteria are missing, or a claimed test was not executed. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A review-specialist prompt requests first-hand filesystem/test observations and VERDICT: PASS|FAIL; the engine parses the verdict and passes it to operator decision/completion guards. - Decision owner: The configured external review-capable CLI model when a reviewer is assigned; the default role contract establishes the auditor-specific path but does not guarantee distinct reviewer identity or universal enforced read-only rights. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: reviewer.md inspection rubric, kind: review delegation, dependency ordering after implementation, extractVerdict, latest-review checks and delivery-proof safeguards. - Closure path: Implementation reports/files → separately invoked reviewer model inspecting files and tests → parsed verdict and findings → operator receives review message and replans, or gated PASS allows completion. - Boundary reachability: In shipped balanced mode, ensureBalancedRoleChain automatically adds a review assignment if a compatible agent exists; specialistPrompt loads the packaged reviewer role and the live runAssignments path parses the result. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The reviewer LLM would choose the finding, but independence is not enforced when the same configured agent is eligible for implementation and review, and the read-only rule is partly prompt-based; therefore the first-party complementary audit constructor is credited as C, without asserting universal closed independent A audit. - Claim being audited: Implementer statement that work meets the task&#39;s acceptance criteria, changes exist and verification evidence is sufficient. - Ordinary reporting path: Implementation agent output and task completion report to the operator. - Complementary access path: Reviewer is instructed to inspect changed files, call sites, tests and diff evidence directly, independently of the implementation report; configured read-only MCP route is optional. - Independence boundary: Separate review delegation/role and verdict, but a distinct underlying CLI identity and truly enforced filesystem read-only access are not mandatory in default configuration. - Who acts on findings: Operator LLM through its review-phase decision and the first-party completion/verification guard. - Evidence: reviewer role, balanced role chain and review handling, MCP routing. - Basis: function-specific first-party audit path; independence gap bounds state at C. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: Reviewer availability depends on installed/configured CLI agents; prompt-only review discipline is not proof of technical read-only confinement.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No demonstrated agent-owned prospective adaptation portfolio/option selection returning into the organization&#39;s current capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing project facts and provider availability are potential environmental changes; CrewCtl handles them as current task execution context or deterministic resilience. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No separate S4 option-development and prospective adaptation right evidenced. - Decision owner: Not established within the credited runtime. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Post-success operator-authored CONTEXT.md revision, per-task memory, scheduled tasks, CLI discovery and provider retry/failover. - Closure path: Context refresh may inform the next task, but no evidenced external/future distinction → adaptation-option judgment → governance/current-capability change closes S4. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: LLM maintenance of facts about work already completed is useful memory, not by itself environmental scanning and prospective adaptation ownership. - Evidence: engine reviseProjectContext and resilience, scheduling. - Basis: bounded structural negative. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: Developers could add an adaptive strategy, but such a possible extension is not first-party evidenced.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No first-party identity/ultimate-policy authority loop is established for the local CrewCtl team. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Risky plans, permissions and unsafe tooling affect ordinary task execution but are not proven existential identity or organization-purpose decisions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No authoritative identity/policy revision decision and returned organizational change evidenced. - Decision owner: User selects goals and grants initial autonomous consent; ordinary per-plan approval does not establish S5 authority at this assessed recursion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: autonomousConsentAcceptedAt, approvalMode, risky keyword patterns, sandbox mode, MCP role gating, CLI overrides and resource budgets. - Closure path: Human ask/approve may unblock a task, but not an identity-level issue → legitimate policy judgment → governing return. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Neither the operator&#39;s task plans nor the human&#39;s runtime consent decides what the CrewCtl organization ultimately is or ought to become. - Evidence: engine consent and plan approval, default config, MCP role filters. - Basis: bounded structural negative. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: External owner and maintainer governance may determine ultimate project identity, but repository presence of those actors does not establish this function&#39;s returned first-party path.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/756e81aee3272ab7002ba760f6c668c318185784/assessments/cr/crewctl.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 756e81aee3272ab7002ba760f6c668c318185784\">756e81a &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-18T14:11:24+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/YahyaHammad/Venastine-Research-Harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Venastine Research Harness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-18\">2026-11-18</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/YahyaHammad/Venastine-Research-Harness/tree/bcf78619b56ec8bffead629673f074b9a0a34a8c\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision bcf78619b56ec8bffead629673f074b9a0a34a8c\">bcf7861 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Carry out conversational and research work under model-selected tool actions and source judgments. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing user questions, evidence quality, tool returns and provider failures. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select tool calls and parameters, synthesize results and choose the next action or final response. - Decision owner: Active chat model or model-driven research pass within the shipped loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Tool authorization, bounded dispatch, conversation storage, pass validation and report persistence. - Closure path: Tool results re-enter the model context and can change subsequent calls or final output. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The dispatcher executes selected calls and returns observations. Removing the model removes substantive discretionary selection. - Boundary reachability: Normal CLI/TUI chat uses the bundled conversation loop, and research mode dispatches model-driven passes through the same harness core. - Evidence: chat entry, model-tool loop, research pipeline. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: Source paths establish standard-distribution reachability. Provider execution was unobserved.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No material agentic inter-unit interference regulator was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Child or concurrent tool work can interact through local resources and may block a parent awaiting completion. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A separate decision right governing mutual adjustment between operational units was not established. - Decision owner: Session supervisor, dispatch grouping and parent-to-child delegation control their individual paths. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Thread ownership, tool grants, bounded scheduling and wake event delivery. - Closure path: A child result or subprocess event returns to its caller or owner thread. The examined paths do not feed a negotiated interference response back to multiple units. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model chooses delegated tasks while grant boundaries and ordering are enforced by the runtime. - Evidence: parallel grouping, child execution, child wake. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: Bounded finding for shipped modes. A deployment could design additional coordination.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: Intervene in present background commitments across the process when local agent work blocks the operator or consumes shared execution capacity. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Live work may block a parent turn via a subprocess owned by a child in another thread. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select which live process-wide commitment to terminate and release its own waiting execution path. - Decision owner: Local TUI operator with process-wide authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Session registry, live sidebar, session-specific selection, worker-side stop execution and wake events. - Closure path: A selected termination changes the target process state. The kill event reaches the owning turn immediately or on its next user message. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Agent tools are scoped to their owner thread while the TUI operator can inspect and intervene across child threads. - Boundary reachability: The local TUI ships the process-wide live-session picker and termination command. - Whole-system current view: The session registry exposes all live subprocess rows across the local process, including child-owned rows. - Current-control decision scope: The operator selects a live commitment to remove while keeping other commitments active and returning the result to the affected turn. - Evidence: operator interruption and wake, whole-process live state, wake payload. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: This parent mode is limited to current subprocess commitments in one local TUI process. The evidence does not establish autonomous portfolio control.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Expose unsupported claims, wrong confidence tiers and report errors through complementary post-run verification. - Disturbance / variety regulated: The ordinary research pipeline can publish a confident claim or summary that the primary source does not support. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Decide independently which claims to check and which concrete corrections to propose. - Decision owner: Bundled pipeline-reviewer model agent. The attended operator decides whether a correction may mutate the result. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Separate reviewer thread, selectable research review, authorized source retrieval and consent records. - Closure path: The reviewer returns structured findings. The operator can accept them and the runner regenerates and persists the report when synthesis succeeds. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Removing the reviewer removes its source checks and substantive findings. Consent gates application after the audit judgment. - Boundary reachability: The shipped research review path invokes the bundled role after synthesis and exposes retrieval tools when authorized. - Claim being audited: Reported claims, tier assignments and report statements faithfully reflect their underlying sources. - Ordinary reporting path: Pipeline-grounded claims, scores, trace and final report. - Complementary access path: The reviewer can retrieve linked publications or search for relevant primary evidence through its own allowed calls. - Independence boundary: A separate review thread reads the finished run without inheriting producer-pass conversation history and can make its own external retrieval calls. - Who acts on findings: The attended operator accepts, rejects or requests refinement, then the pipeline applies the accepted corrective judgment through re-synthesis. - Evidence: reviewer role and source tools, review input and separate run, findings and corrective synthesis, attended review entry. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: Credit is limited to the optional authorized source-verification mode. A tool-less review sees only delivered artifacts and the same provider can power the producer and reviewer.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No material shipped adaptation loop turns prospective external developments into changes to the harness&#39;s own repertoire. - Disturbance / variety regulated: New research evidence changes reports while evolving model capability and operating conditions could demand changes to the harness itself. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No decisive outside-and-then adaptation right was found. - Decision owner: The operator chooses providers/configuration and supplies research queries. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Research, source retrieval, remembered context, provider settings and capability controls. - Closure path: Retrieved evidence updates the current task output. Remembered information conditions later prompts without selecting a prospective system adaptation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The models choose research actions and proposed findings inside preselected operating modes. - Evidence: research purpose and phases, memory injection, provider and runtime settings. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No material shipped identity-level adjudication path was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Tool access, workspace trust and project instructions must remain within operator-defined bounds. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No system identity or ultimate-policy decision routes back into durable operational governance through a function-specific path. - Decision owner: Operator and distribution configuration define permitted modes and tools. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Static trust/access rules, scoped permissions, role prompts and per-run approval. - Closure path: Selected rules shape ordinary invocation and review consent applies a specific correction. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The agent selects work inside constraints while the shipped controls act on narrower tool, workspace and task decisions. - Evidence: permission and workspace model, workspace and grants, role definitions. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: Workspace trust can govern imported project context. The examined trust route addresses which project inputs and tools the operating mode permits.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/882b48c1f0f6a5a753ed5025327b1ae71146eed1/assessments/ve/venastine-research-harness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 882b48c1f0f6a5a753ed5025327b1ae71146eed1\">882b48c &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-20T04:21:03+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Hyper-AI-Lab/cyber-ai-team\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Cyber AI Team</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-20\">2026-10-20</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Hyper-AI-Lab/cyber-ai-team/tree/525401e40f34863cc7f4d3b053d437ce7709912f\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 525401e40f34863cc7f4d3b053d437ce7709912f\">525401e &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Continuing, bounded AI business-domain operations: domain-role agents process routed company events and enduring mandate backlog work, reason from current company evidence, propose actions and follow-up tasks, and return durable outcomes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Incoming CRM/finance/support/operations events, conflicting or stale role/memory claims, evidence gaps, unavailable tools/providers, workflow failures and limits on action authority. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Registered, configured model agents in AgentManager.invokeagent choose structured assessments/recommendedaction/proposedwork or one of trusted action-candidate references using the first-party LLMGateway. The domain loop leases assigned work, invokes model analysis, grounds its claims and may create evidence-bound follow-up work or request an owner escalation. Direct external side effects are mediated by separate action policy/approval. - Decision owner: First-party AI role bound by durable AgentMandate and DomainAutonomyControl; the LLM owns semantic bounded work choices, while deterministic WorkPortfolio validates scope, evidence and persistence and the parent owner controls high-impact authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: RoleManifest/Agent records, model gateway, memory protocol and authoritative ERPNext/context snapshots, per-domain mandate and work leases, WorkPortfolioService.executeadvisorywork, grounded output schema and follow-up creation, outcome service. - Closure path: ERPNext/other company signal or continuing domain mandate → BusinessEvent router assigns a BusinessWorkItem → domain-role LLM assesses current trusted company context and recommends work/action → grounding/action-policy gate → task result, new work, evidence, owner attention or blocked decision → returned domain-loop state/next cycles. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Packaged default role families, AgentManager LLM invoke and WorkPortfolioService.runalldomainloops/executeadvisorywork are wired into AutonomousCompanyCycleService.run. A configured supported real LLM provider is required; the decisive role task choice is not merely a static role inventory or offline test fixture. - Evidence: Without a model role&#39;s current evidence-based reasoning the system still could route events and manage leases, but could not autonomously decide nuanced domain next work and trusted candidate selection; this is source-backed S1 inside limited authority. - Basis: backend/src/cyberteam/agents/manager.py; backend/src/cyberteam/llm/gateway.py; backend/src/cyberteam/operations/workportfolio.py; backend/src/cyberteam/operations/autonomycycle.py; backend/src/cyberteam/roles/defaults.yaml; backend/src/cyberteam/operations/actionpolicy.py; backend/src/cyberteam/memory/protocol.py. - Confidence: First-party business-domain LLM work execution code rather than claims from diagrams or hypothetical integrations. - Caveats: High for agent-owned advisory/internal domain S1; no live ERPNext/provider deployment benchmark. - undefined: Most first-party model-produced outputs are advisory and some safe actions can be applied only after detailed policy checks. This assessment does not assume blanket autonomous external purchases, messages, trades or legal authority. - Boundary reachability: backend/src/cyberteam/agents/manager.py; backend/src/cyberteam/operations/workportfolio.py; backend/src/cyberteam/operations/autonomycycle.py: the first-party production run chain registers actual model-driven agents, invokes them on continuing role queues and stores/feeds their results back to business-domain operation.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No separate evidence-backed agent-owned mutual disturbance-damping function between ongoing viable S1 domains, despite real first-party global event routing, duplicate suppression and per-domain backlog caps. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Company events may affect finance, support, engineering and sales in different ways; duplicate work, growing task queues and resource contention risk oscillations or work collisions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: First-party WorkPortfolio routes classified events to domains and deterministically suppresses duplicate work, excessive proposal depth and per-domain backlog overflow. This is shared task admission/load shedding under fixed rules; it does not delegate independent S2 judgment to negotiate conflicts or return reciprocal compromises between distinct operating domain units. - Decision owner: Deterministic router, queue lease/capacity/stabilizer and host configuration, not a separately authorized inter-S1 coordinating decision owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: DomainRegistry mappings, routependingevents, stabilizedomainbacklogs, BusinessEventDelivery, role mandates, domain capacity controls and work idempotency. - Closure path: Event→one routed role work item; over-limit or semantically duplicate proposal→deterministic cancellation; task result→work status. No distinct domain-vs-domain interference report→S2 negotiation/decision→modified reciprocal commitments to affected domain roles. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Continual domain roles and mandates are more than a one-shot DAG, so S2 was explicitly tested. But control acts as deterministic per-event/per-domain bookkeeping, not an agent owned cross-domain disturbance regulator with evidentially returned decisions to multiple units. - Evidence: backend/src/cyberteam/operations/workportfolio.py; backend/src/cyberteam/company/domainregistry.py; backend/src/cyberteam/operations/operatingmodel.py; backend/src/cyberteam/operations/governor.py; backend/src/cyberteam/operations/autonomycycle.py. - Basis: Bounded negative after inspecting highest-potential cross-domain shared queue and stabilizer. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: The product may help owners coordinate domains manually or compose later arbitration. Neither is credited as the independent autonomous S2 ownership mode in this pinned source.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: Inside-and-now company current control of independently continuing S1 business domains: owner chooses which business domain may operate, be paused or taken over and returns the decision to domain-worker execution; parent-governed operational mode is closed. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unsafe autonomous operations in a domain, misconfigured capability/role, changing trust in company evidence, required human takeover, conflicts of authority, failed domain activity or unacceptable risk. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Authenticated owner makes binding per-domain active | paused | takeover decision with a reason, recorded as DomainControlRevision and DomainAutonomyControl; subsequent rundomainloop explicitly prevents work when state is not active, and runalldomainloops admits only active domain roles. The owner has a present operational intervention right across continuing domain S1s, not merely a generic approve-this-tool button. - Decision owner: Legitimate authenticated human company owner through a first-party PUT /domain-controls/{domain} route requiring resource-specific authorization, WorkPortfolioService.updatedomaincontrol and persistent owner revisions. Automated operating model reconciler / governor enforce policy and state but do not themselves own a separate evidenced discretionary autonomous S3; publication P. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Principal/getcurrentprincipal and requireauthorization, DomainControlRevision and DomainAutonomyControl, owner lock, WorkPortfolioService and per-domain run loop, resumption signals and audit control evidence; deterministic government snapshots and role status. - Closure path: Owner observes operating domain/roles/readiness from console → sends authenticated active/paused/takeover decision with cause → service records owner-locked current control revision and effective domain state → later domain task leasing stops or resumes, and domain state is auditable/visible → owner can revise decision. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Parent mode can run within the shipped self-hosted company OS: the API route directly calls updatedomaincontrol, commits owner-marked revision, and the first-party continuing domain-role loop checks effective control before leasing work. No unpublished owner-agent code is needed. - Evidence: A fixed router/threshold is not by itself S3; the distinctive decisive choice here is the owner&#39;s discretionary current intervention over multiple persistent autonomous operating domains, with a first-party returned effect on their operation. - Basis: backend/src/cyberteam/api/routes/operations.py; backend/src/cyberteam/operations/workportfolio.py; backend/src/cyberteam/operations/operatingmodel.py; backend/src/cyberteam/db/models.py; backend/src/cyberteam/operations/executive.py; backend/src/cyberteam/authorization/service.py. - Confidence: Specific first-party parent decision/return at existing multi-domain operational recursion, separate from governance merely via approval buttons. - Caveats: High for closed S3=P intervention mode; not an S3=A claim. - undefined: Authorization ensures an authenticated principal with permission; actual company ownership and legitimate policy are deployment supplied. The control is domain active/pause/takeover and is not proof of unrestricted owner allocation of financial budgets or every company-wide executive decision. - Boundary reachability: backend/src/cyberteam/api/routes/operations.py; backend/src/cyberteam/operations/workportfolio.py: authenticated owner domain-control decision persisted in actual revision/effective control and consumed by continuing role work loops; supported mode is packaged API/service, not only a suggested integration. - Whole-system current view: The owner console and listdomaincontrols expose the whole registered operating-domain set, effective states and revision IDs; the Governor snapshot/operating model adds mandates, workflows, gaps and current readiness. - Current-control decision scope: Binding owner decision to pause, take over or release continuing business-domain S1 work; this changes immediate operating authorization rather than merely reporting state or limiting a single task&#39;s tool. - Parent-mode decisive owner: Authenticated company owner using a resource-scoped authorized HTTP operation, not the deterministic decide Governor rules or a model recommendation. - Parent-mode trigger and closure: Owner observes unacceptable/current domain condition → chooses active/paused/takeover with reason → saved DomainControlRevision (owner-locked) → per-domain loop blocks/allows future work, confirmed in list/readiness and audit → owner can revise the state. - Mode distinction: Parent-governed P only; no separate autonomous S3 A or autonomous-actor-composition S3 C is asserted at this boundary.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No autonomous complementary S3 audit with independent direct operational reality access challenging the owner/current-management account and returning a corrective management conclusion, despite substantive Observer/data consistency checks. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Governor may make poor decisions from incomplete snapshots, company strategy proposals may rely on stale claims, role models may assert incorrect current staff/ERPNext state, and action approvals may be unsafe. - Decisive decision or feedback right: ObserverReview checks proposed strategy/operating model against saved evidence, schemas, authority and model confidence and can block changes; grounding verifier can quarantine contradictions with trusted role records. These inspect governed proposals and internal reports, not independently commission a complementary test of S3 parent&#39;s entire current-state claims against a separate field reality route, then deliver enforceable correction to the owner. - Decision owner: Deterministic Observer validators and first-party authoritative-state/claims gates. ObserverAgent is named, but this code does not establish an independently authorized S3 auditor with distinct field observation and return to parent S3. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: OperatingModelLifecycleService.reviewrevision and reviewfindings, CompanyStrategyService.recordobserverreview, executive ObserverReview, WorkPortfolio claim grounding, memory conflict quarantine, read-only audit/event storage. - Closure path: Proposed operating model/strategy → Observer validates same persisted evidence and marks agreed or ownerreview → reconciliation blocked/allowed. S3 owner domain control remains separately managed; no independent S3 report test at actual domain operations→audit finding→binding owner S3 corrective response. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A second deterministic look at the same data can guard unsafe plans and remain operationally useful; audit independence and complementary access to current organizational field truth are not proven by a separate &#39;Observer&#39; class or approval gate. - Evidence: backend/src/cyberteam/operations/operatingmodel.py; backend/src/cyberteam/operations/strategy.py; backend/src/cyberteam/operations/executive.py; backend/src/cyberteam/operations/workportfolio.py; backend/src/cyberteam/audit/service.py; backend/src/cyberteam/operations/supervisorreview.py. - Basis: Bounded absence after reviewing all plausible independent validation and critical-observer modes. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: An independent real-world evaluator could use the saved evidence or ERPNext data, but is not shown as shipped S3 mandate and owner-return.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: Outside-and-then prospective company intelligence and adaptation: acquire/research changing external company evidence, generate measurable strategic objectives/KPIs/experiments and feed accepted strategy into the next actual business operating model and mandates. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unknown market/customer/offerings facts, changes in external company evidence, emerging company roles/capability gaps, KPI performance drift and changing future priorities. - Decisive decision or feedback right: First-party CompanyIntelligenceService acquires evidence, builds an active company model and resolves unknowns; CompanyStrategyService invokes LLM to propose future objectives, KPI formulas and reversible experiments from provenance claims, validates proposals, subjects them to Observer gate, then commits active/probationary revisions and retires stale alternatives. Subsequent OperatingModelLifecycleService synthesizes active objectives and KPI revisions and reconciles business domains/role mandates. - Decision owner: Model-backed Strategy Portfolio Agent owns proposed future option content; validating Observer and deterministic policy own admission constraints; authorized human parent remains owner of high-impact external actions. The first-party agent-driven future option selection/feedback loop is closed for low-risk proposed strategy without an additional user request. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Evidence acquisition/discovery/research, CompanyModelRevision, LLM strategy proposals and validation, observer activation verdict, objective/KPI/experiment revision writes, current state reuse, operating model source hash and reconcile/convergerolesandmandates, periodic autonomous company cycle. - Closure path: Company/ERPNext/public external evidence → active company model/claims → model produces forward-looking discovery/strategic goals and reversible experiments → evidence+Observer validation accepts or escalates → active strategy revisions stored → next operatingmodel.synthesize consumes revisions and reconciles actual domains and role mandates → domain loops receive changed work/capabilities. Same source event no-ops on unchanged strategy context hash. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The published AutonomousCompanyCycleService.run calls acquisition, discovery, strategy.runstrategycycle, operatingmodel.synthesizeandreview/reconcile/convergerolesandmandates, and runalldomainloops in that order; this is a tangible end-to-end return path from model-selected future options into continuing operations. - Evidence: Unlike static cron triggers or one-case retrospective memory, the LLM chooses new strategic objectives/experiments on separately acquired company evidence and the code installs accepted objective revisions into the company operating-model source of truth. - Basis: backend/src/cyberteam/operations/autonomycycle.py; backend/src/cyberteam/company/intelligence.py; backend/src/cyberteam/operations/strategy.py; backend/src/cyberteam/operations/operatingmodel.py; backend/src/cyberteam/operations/workportfolio.py; backend/src/cyberteam/db/models.py. - Confidence: First-party model-assisted future option selection, evidence gate, durable adoption and operational return; does not assert quality of predictions. - Caveats: High for source-reachable S4=A (conditional on a configured provider and active verified company model). - undefined: Active model and Observer agreement are hard prerequisites; failed inference or uncertain provenance blocks option admission. Not evidence of live external market intelligence success or high financial returns. Owner-approved high impact external changes are separate and no distinct S4(P) mode asserted. - Boundary reachability: backend/src/cyberteam/operations/autonomycycle.py; backend/src/cyberteam/operations/strategy.py; backend/src/cyberteam/company/intelligence.py; backend/src/cyberteam/operations/operatingmodel.py: real first-party scheduled chain acquires external evidence, proposes and accepts future objectives, persists revisions and applies them to ongoing role/domain operating capability. - External distinction: CompanyIntelligenceService.acquireavailableevidence and researchmodelunknowns create versioned company reality/claim inputs using supported ERPNext and public-source evidence; they are separate from current task execution. - Future / prospective distinction: LLM proposes strategic objectives with targets, KPI bindings and reversible experiments for future operating conditions; not just a retry of a failed task. - Adaptation option generated: New objective/KPI/experiment revisions proposed by LLM, validated against observed claims and source hash, subject to Observer acceptance or owner-review gate. - Path back into current capability / S3: Accepted revisions are persisted and included in OperatingModelLifecycleService.synthesize; subsequent reconcile/convergerolesandmandates updates domain state and role authority, then continuing WorkPortfolio domain loops execute work in the new current system. Owner S3=P can independently pause/release affected domains.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No first-party closed ultimate identity or constitutional-policy decision despite legitimate human owner controls, global AutonomyPolicy thresholds, default business objectives, approval APIs and FOSS-only resource rules. - Disturbance / variety regulated: The company may change mission/purpose, legal owner responsibilities, financial exposure thresholds, acceptable risk and external-service legitimacy. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Owner-authenticated APIs can set operational domain states, approve actions, update strategy/objective targets and change executive resource/mode thresholds; however no first-party identity/charter issue → legitimate ultimate company purpose judgment → binding institution-wide constitution/identity return path is demonstrated as distinct from current S3 interventions or future S4 portfolio revisions. - Decision owner: External legitimate human owner defines overarching company mission, deployment permissions, policy settings and company model; first-party services enforce changed operational rules, not a shipped S5 constitutional governance function with its own decision record. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ExecutiveCompanyOSService.updatepolicy/replaceobjectives, role approval/authorization gates, company owner console/API, default guardrails and FOSS resource inventory, domain mandates. - Closure path: Owner or developer config → updated current operating thresholds, company KPI/objective data or action approval → authorized work. No explicit S5 identity settlement/mission/purpose charter decision record with binding next-cycle internal policy above S3/S4; these are operation/strategy configuration tools. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Legitimate owner is present and S3=P is proven, but an owner editing defaults or approving a tool is not automatically S5=P. The functions and their decision/return must be distinguished at the organizational identity layer. - Evidence: backend/src/cyberteam/operations/executive.py; backend/src/cyberteam/api/routes/operations.py; backend/src/cyberteam/authorization/service.py; backend/src/cyberteam/operations/actionpolicy.py; README.md; backend/src/cyberteam/operations/operatingmodel.py. - Basis: Bounded no-evidence at first-party ultimate-policy layer after inspecting strongest parent pathways. - Confidence: Medium. - Caveats: A separate corporate constitution/owner mandate could exist in real deployment. This source review establishes concrete S3 parent domain powers, not a distinct source-backed S5 constitutional decision procedure.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/96319ae4f89049b559aebcd43cebf5534fa332a2/assessments/cy/cyber-ai-team.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 96319ae4f89049b559aebcd43cebf5534fa332a2\">96319ae &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-18T16:36:29+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/awslabs/AutoHelix\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AutoHelix</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-18\">2026-11-18</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/awslabs/AutoHelix/tree/7ccc11341f16cfaa9039945b8bcb67684dba7a34\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 7ccc11341f16cfaa9039945b8bcb67684dba7a34\">7ccc113 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Make and iteratively improve project changes with a model-selected coding strategy. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Defects, performance or feature demands against a changing source state and observed checks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose concrete edits and iteration strategy, including using rejected patches, prior metrics and notes to decide subsequent project actions. - Decision owner: Configured model coding agent launched and scoped by the first-party AutoHelix harness. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: CLI adapter, isolated agent copy, frozen editable scope, prompt with iteration feedback, check/observe pipeline, run ref and iteration records. - Closure path: Agent edits project copy; AutoHelix captures its tree and checks, accepts validated outcomes to the run ref or records failure, then returns metrics/rejected diffs/feedback to the next agent iteration and optionally lands accepted work. - Boundary reachability: Shipped autohelix run/resume with a configured supported coding-agent CLI launches the operational loop; external inference is a normal dependency, not an external orchestrator replacing the installed harness. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The agent selects substantive code/task interventions. The host&#39;s deterministic accept/deny rules constrain the agent&#39;s decisions but do not author them. - Evidence: agent CLI invocation, attempt/evaluation/accept cycle, project protocol, iterative artifact behavior. - Basis: structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: A configured functioning Claude Code/Codex/OpenCode runtime is required. This review inspected source and contract, not a paid live optimization run.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No independently evidenced conflict-attenuating relation between distinct current S1 runs; group peer visibility alone does not suffice. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Group members editing shared project source can compete for the same files or produce mutually incompatible changes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A coordinator would need to resolve an actual inter-run conflict or conflict-derived priority and feed an attenuating choice back to each affected S1. - Decision owner: Group runs independently select changes and have separate budgets; no S2-specific agent/controller is instantiated by membership. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Group membership, read-only peer snapshots, notes/history, frozen scopes and independent per-run Git refs. - Closure path: Peer code can be copied and rechecked, but no first-party conflict arbitration or coordination feedback changes both operations as a result of their interference. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Sharing peers&#39; notes/refs, lock safety and manual per-run landing do not themselves establish an attenuating S2 relationship between competing S1 agents. - Evidence: group semantics, peer snapshots in workspace, run isolation and conflict warning - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: Parent operator manages current project-wide work commitments and the choice of which checked output enters the branch. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Multiple live runs can consume budget, diverge, miss targets or propose competing project results under time pressure. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Based on the current run portfolio, select a run to stop, alter its next-iteration direction with hint, resume it later or land a chosen validated winner rather than another run. - Decision owner: Legitimate local project operator with authority over project run commands and target Git branch. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Project-wide run table/JSON viewer with live state, cost, progress/metrics and landing status; per-run stop request, appended hint file, manual land/archival and fast-forward checks. - Closure path: Viewer reports run state and measurements; operator stops a selected run or appends a hint consumed in its next iteration, or lands a chosen checked tip into the project; runtime returns the authoritative change into future operational runs/branch. - Boundary reachability: Installed CLI supports view without a run ID, stop RUNID..., hint --run ID and land RUNID on that same project checkout; group comparison and manual landing are documented operating modes. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The operator, not the coding agent or budget checker, makes project-wide intervention and acceptance choices; autonomous S3 is not established. - Whole-system current view: Project-wide multi-run view exposes active/finished run IDs, phase, costs, metrics, group state and checked tip&#39;s landing condition at the assessed project recursion. - Current-control decision scope: Stop or steer currently running S1 commitments and choose between validated results; landing modifies the current project&#39;s code basis for subsequent work. - Evidence: project-wide viewer, stop and returned state, hint writing and next-run consumption, prompt hint inclusion, run comparison and winner landing. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: Parent-controlled mode applies to an attended project with multiple runs and their same-project branch; it is not an autonomous portfolio controller. The frontend is read-only; interventions are through CLI.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Independently inspect a coding agent&#39;s candidate change against an operator-defined correctness or policy question before it is accepted. - Disturbance / variety regulated: A coding agent can produce plausible but wrong changes, including changes gaming ordinary tests or corrupting the check substrate. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Separate read-only model judge decides a yes/no question with evidence about the whole candidate and its diff; a no/invalid/failed gate causes the candidate&#39;s rejection and returns result details. - Decision owner: Distinct first-party launched read-only judge model session configured via built-in ask gate. The first-party harness enforces the returned gate verdict. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Separate checked checkout of frozen candidate, independent judge system prompt, JSON answer schema, read-only tools, DirectoryGuard mutation check, stable gate configuration and per-iteration evaluation. - Closure path: Configured ask gate calls a new judge with candidate copy, receives a structured independent verdict; gate exit status enters evaluation and rejects incorrect changes, preserving reason/patch for the next S1 attempt. The judge cannot rewrite the candidate. - Boundary reachability: Shipped protocol supports an ask gate; autohelix ask and judgeconfig are built-in and invoked by the run&#39;s gate evaluator in a fresh check copy with separate authorization from the editing agent. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The audit judgment comes from a distinct model-run reviewer with its own instructions and read-only access. Code running fixed tests is additional enforcement, not the basis of positive S3. - Claim being audited: Whether the coding agent&#39;s changed code satisfies the selected question (e.g. API compatibility or correctness) without altering the check target. - Ordinary reporting path: Editing agent&#39;s final output, change summary and gate/benchmark observations used to select progress. - Complementary access path: Independent judge&#39;s fresh, read-only candidate snapshot and complete diff; no producer conversation or agent-owned notes are supplied to the judge. - Independence boundary: Distinct judge process, system instructions and model call, immutable candidate snapshot, read-only tools and file mutation guard; optional distinct model family. The configured human question scopes what is audited. - Who acts on findings: Harness check gate rejects a no/failed verdict, and S1 receives rejected attempt reason/patch; project operator sees gate output through the viewer. - Evidence: built-in configurable ask gate, judge isolation and read-only agent, question and read-only config, actual judge run and write guard, candidate evaluation/rejection, documented reviews versus gates. - Basis: structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: This positive is mode-specific: requires an installed judge-compatible provider (Claude Code or Codex) and an operator-defined ask gate; without one, ordinary deterministic checks and optional non-gating reviews cannot be credited as S3. A read-only LLM reviewer is fallible.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No distinct outside-and-future strategic sensing and system-capability adaptation loop is demonstrated. - Disturbance / variety regulated: An evolving external environment could make project goals, provider strategy or constraints obsolete. - Decisive decision or feedback right: An S4 actor would identify external prospective distinctions and author a new capability or strategic direction that is applied to later operations. - Decision owner: Model agent improves current code under a static user-defined goal; operator may initiate a fresh run, not a shipped distinct S4 pathway. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Metrics, previous review counts, agent notes, group sharing, model feedback, run import and project protocol. - Closure path: The next iteration improves the current goal and consumes prior results, but no outside-future signal is translated into an authoritative capability adaptation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Iterative performance improvement and use of previous reviews are S1 improvement under current intent, not evidence of prospective environmental intelligence and returned S4. - Evidence: iteration feedback, review previous state, config protocol - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: The configured goal/scope/gates govern an optimization task but do not demonstrate a distinct ultimate policy or identity-issue decision function at the project-system recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Project-wide identity constraints and organizational mandates might conflict with locally chosen optimization goals. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A legitimate S5 authority would adjudicate a distinct identity/ultimate-policy issue and return a binding decision governing project operations. - Decision owner: Human project owner authors goals and acceptance thresholds, but no distinct S5 policy-issue review/decision path is established by the shipped harness. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Committed YAML goal, editable/frozen scope, test gates, budget, landing choice and operator run commands. - Closure path: A new run copies the chosen protocol and enforces its constraints; no identity-policy conflict decision and returned ultimate mandate is operationally established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A static task contract and config immutability are important constraints, but are not alone a first-party policy/identity closure process. - Evidence: protocol goal/scope/gates, immutability and run selection, gate contract - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/30d00889fba2c4a62b72928ad0230b516391d1c4/assessments/au/autohelix.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 30d00889fba2c4a62b72928ad0230b516391d1c4\">30d0088 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-04T18:29:24+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/christianhelle/puny\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Puny</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-04\">2026-10-04</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/christianhelle/puny/tree/dcdecb253b81415152aac4d3590d2186adfa0c81\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision dcdecb253b81415152aac4d3590d2186adfa0c81\">dcdecb2 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform coding work against the local project by interpreting a user task, selecting built-in repository/tool actions, changing source and observing results until the requested outcome is reached. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous codebases and tasks, incomplete local context, tool/provider failures, command/test results, Git state, file contents, context-window pressure and reviewer findings during orchestrated repair. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose which project evidence/tool to use next, what source changes to make, how to react to tool/check results and when the coding task is complete. - Decision owner: the configured model-backed Puny coding actor operating through the first-party native chat/tool loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: mode-specific tool registry; filesystem/shell/search/Git/web/skill tools; provider transports; session persistence; context compaction; retry handling; Git backstop commits; prompts and CLI/session lifecycle. - Closure path: task + repository/session state → model chooses tool/action → Puny executes against the environment → concrete result/error returns into the conversation → model revises the work or completes. - Boundary reachability: the normal supported CLI directly instantiates this model/tool loop with local or hosted provider choices; no external agent framework must supply the operational loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: if the model actor is removed while deterministic tools/session storage remain, open-ended coding judgment about what to inspect, change and do next disappears. - Evidence: src/main.zig; src/chat/chat.zig; src/chat/session.zig; src/tools/root.zig; README. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: model inference is provider-supplied, but provider transport is a dependency; Puny concretely instantiates and governs the actor/tool feedback loop.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material first-party coordination function among multiple same-recursion production S1 units was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Puny intentionally ships without subagents or first-party parallel worker organization; README directs users who want parallel work to run another independent instance. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: single-session mode state; Git branch scope; one orchestrate phase at a time; provider/session identifiers. - Closure path: no distinct production-S1 interference → coordination response → changed peer behavior path exists in the shipped organization. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: fresh implement/review/fix contexts are sequential roles around one operation, while the reviewer is an audit role rather than a peer production S1. Independently launched Puny processes are not coordinated by Puny. - Evidence: README; src/chat/orchestrate.zig; src/tools/root.zig. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: plurality of phases/models is not sufficient for S2 without multiple operational S1s and a concrete interference relation.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no distinct whole-system current-control function over a portfolio of operational S1 commitments/resources was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: orchestrate iteration bounds, phase transitions, session state, context budgets and review outcomes regulate one focal coding operation rather than several operational units. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established at S3 level. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: orchestrate state progression; max iterations; write blocking by mode; session/context budgets; Git preflight/backstop commits; retry/error handling. - Closure path: these mechanisms bound or sequence the current task; no whole-system current view plus substantive resource/priority/commitment intervention across multiple S1s exists. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the orchestrate controller is deterministic phase machinery. It enforces the implement/review/fix protocol but does not exercise discretionary current management over an operational portfolio. - Evidence: src/chat/orchestrate.zig; src/core/session.zig; src/chat/session.zig; src/review/review.zig. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a strong autonomous workflow controller is not automatically VSM S3.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently audit the committed branch produced by the coding actor, determine whether it is safe/complete enough to merge, and return blocking findings into a corrective implementation/re-review loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: implementation defects, regressions, compatibility/security/performance/error-handling problems, missing tests/docs, failed checks and false confidence by the implementing model. - Decisive decision or feedback right: a fresh model-backed reviewer independently decides whether evidence is complete and whether the immutable committed branch range is merge-worthy, while identifying concrete findings and recommended fixes. - Decision owner: the separate fresh-context Puny review actor. Deterministic report validation, write blocking and outcome conversion enforce the review contract but do not supply the substantive audit judgment. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: beginPhase(.review) context reset; dedicated review system prompt; freshly fetched origin/main; immutable merge-base..HEAD scope; review-only read/check tool registry; global source-write block; savereviewresults; host validation of required sections/evidence flags; bounded orchestrate iterations. - Closure path: build actor commits implementation → Puny resets context and starts read-only reviewer over immutable committed range → reviewer directly inspects changed/surrounding files and can run relevant checks → reviewer saves evidence + merge-worthiness verdict → rejection writes review-results.md → fresh fix actor is explicitly instructed to open the report and resolve every finding → fixes are committed → another fresh review runs; approval terminates the loop as merge-worthy. - Boundary reachability: both /review/--review and /orchestrate/--orchestrate are shipped first-party CLI modes. Orchestrate directly wires the reviewer verdict into repair/re-review without user intervention. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the review model while retaining report-format validation, immutable Git scope and read-only enforcement removes the open-ended judgment about what is wrong, what evidence to gather and whether the branch is merge-worthy. The deterministic host can only validate/store an already chosen verdict. - Evidence: src/chat/orchestrate.zig; src/review/review.zig; src/tools/root.zig; src/tools/review.zig; src/prompts/prompts.zig; README. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: implement, review and fix may use the same configured provider/model family, but the audit has a fresh conversation, distinct review-only prompt/tool authority, immutable committed evidence scope and source-write prohibition; independence is role/context/evidence separation rather than provider diversity. - Claim being audited: that the current committed feature branch is merge-worthy relative to the freshly resolved origin/main baseline. - Ordinary reporting path: the build actor edits/commits source and could otherwise finish after its own checks and final response. - Complementary access path: the fresh review actor receives the immutable branch/base scope, directly reads project evidence and surrounding code, and can run read-only build/test/lint/check commands rather than trusting the implementer&#39;s self-report. - Independence boundary: review starts after a context reset, excludes the implement/orchestrate build prompt, blocks source mutation, exposes a distinct tool allowlist and scopes its verdict to committed Git evidence. It can reject work the implementer considered complete. - Who acts on findings: a subsequent fresh build-mode model context receives the generated report path and explicit instruction to resolve every listed finding; the repaired branch is then re-submitted to another fresh review.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material autonomous outside-and-future intelligence/adaptation loop was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: durable sessions, saved PRDs, skills, web fetch, context compaction and provider/model selection improve or preserve current work, but they do not autonomously redesign persistent Puny capability/strategy for future operations. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established at S4 level. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: session message persistence/resume; planning artifacts; skills loading; web fetch; context compaction; provider/model picker; review-derived current-task repair. - Closure path: no environmental/future sensing → selected persistent adaptation → later-operation behavior change loop was found. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: retained conversation/project instructions and reusable user-authored skills are context/capability inputs, not an autonomous prospective self-adaptation process. - Evidence: README; src/chat/persistence.zig; src/chat/session.zig; src/skills/; provider/model selection surfaces. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Puny can fetch current web evidence during a task, but current-task research is not by itself S4.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material identity/ultimate-policy decision loop was established at the assessed runtime recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: mode restrictions, system prompts, tool allowlists, source-write blocks, provider configuration and commit discipline constrain execution but remain operating/safety/process policy. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no qualifying identity-level conflict/proposal is decided by a legitimate ultimate authority and returned as binding organizational governance. - Decision owner: not established at S5 level. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: build/planning/review modes; static prompts; tool registries; write-block flag; CLI/config choices; Git branch preconditions; commit rules. - Closure path: no identity/ultimate-policy issue → legitimate authority decision → returned governance → changed subsequent operation loop was found. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the coding/review actors operate within preconfigured host rules; they do not own or resolve the system&#39;s identity/ultimate policy. - Evidence: src/prompts/prompts.zig; src/tools/root.zig; src/core/session.zig; CLI/configuration surfaces. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: review veto and write blocking are strong governance mechanisms, but they govern artifact progression rather than a genuine S5 policy/identity question.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/6d8b2b615d62912d550a7a142eadcfc6acd32058/assessments/puny.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 6d8b2b615d62912d550a7a142eadcfc6acd32058\">6d8b2b6 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-18T18:07:05+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/slopus/happy-agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Happy Agent</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-18\">2026-11-18</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/slopus/happy-agent/tree/bd2bd009c30ca7202f793bd9189ecadc495aeae7\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision bd2bd009c30ca7202f793bd9189ecadc495aeae7\">bd2bd00 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Perform requested project tasks through a native model-backed agent choosing substantive tool and subtask work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Open-ended task requests, source changes, tool results, provider feedback and interruptions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select coding/tool actions and subsequent task steps after receiving first-party executed tool results. - Decision owner: First-party AgentBase model session, not the daemon&#39;s fixed scheduling or an optional external ACP agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Pi/model transport, AgentSystemLocal lifecycle, compute tools, session persistence, permission mode and tool-result commit. - Closure path: AgentBase collects model tool calls, invokes tools and commits outcomes into durable conversation state; further model turns act on returned results and clients receive events. - Boundary reachability: Standard installed daemon starts native provider-backed sessions with native prompt and tools without requiring external vendor whole-agent delegation; desktop/API clients connect to the daemon. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The native model chooses actual task-specific tool actions; first-party substrate constrains execution and feeds results back without replacing the decision. - Evidence: installed first-party and foreign modes, agent system creation/restore, native AgentBase tool invocation and commit, native model response/tool collection. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: Peer inboxes and per-agent locks communicate or serialize one agent&#39;s own lifecycle but do not establish a first-party inter-S1 conflict-attenuation decision. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Two agents may compete for source files, resources or commitments across the same workspace. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A controller would need to detect their specific interference, decide conflict resolution and return affected task constraints. - Decision owner: Task author/agent may message another peer; no S2-specific arbitration owner was evidenced. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: PeerMessagingModule, Task membership, Teammates tools, per-agent locks and session-ownership checks. - Closure path: Messages reach peer inboxes and independently running agents may react, but no shipped conflict witness, selected resolution or binding returned coordination of competing S1s is shown. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: P2P delivery, per-agent mutual exclusion and user task membership are not independently proven inter-agent disturbance attenuation. - Evidence: peer module boundary, per-agent locking, team loop-in - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: Parent controls the current project portfolio of several agent sessions and intervenes in live work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Active tasks can become unsafe, obsolete or conflicting with a human project&#39;s changing current commitments. - Decisive decision or feedback right: From a project/workspace agent collection and focused agent state, choose which run to abort, which mode/goal to adjust and when to resume or retire tasks. - Decision owner: Legitimate project owner/operator through authenticated installed daemon client/API; not a separately claimed automatic project-wide manager. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: API owner-agent series for projects/workspaces, durable task records, abort route with expectedRunId conflict guard, permission mode per session, goal pause/resume. - Closure path: Project-wide agent records and focused run state inform operator choice; the abort endpoint checks active run identity, invokes the abort system and returns the new focused agent resource; later task behavior is altered. - Boundary reachability: Supported first-party daemon project/workspace and task APIs with per-agent abort endpoint and user-facing project controls; not a test harness or external provider console. - Whole-system current view: First-party project/workspace owner-agent series and task associations enumerate current agents, with focused current run information. - Current-control decision scope: Operator can terminate a chosen active run and change task or permission state for subsequent operations; controls apply across multiple agents under that project&#39;s authority. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The operator exercises the portfolio management choice; abort dispatch and API state updates are enforcement, not autonomous management judgement. - Evidence: persistent agents and local system, project/workspace owner agent series, focused abort with conflict guard, persisted goals and modes. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: P applies only to an attended project with multiple agents and client control. No autonomous S3 loop or fleet-level optimization is claimed; API status may vary by installed module config.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Separately inspect and decide the risk/authorization of a native agent&#39;s proposed sensitive operational action before execution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: A coding agent may attempt a workspace/network/shell action inconsistent with user intent or safety restrictions and ordinary self-report is insufficient. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A private reviewer model judges the pending action against user history and independently readable local evidence, may deny a call or grant bounded temporary elevation; refusal is returned to agent&#39;s tool feedback. - Decision owner: A distinct private read-only model reviewer in AutoModule&#39;s isolated AgentSystemLocal; PermissionsModule enforces verdict and fails closed when unavailable. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Separate private storage/lock/provider catalog and reviewer ID, uncompacted user/action transcript provenance, read-only inspection tools, permission reviewer budget and typed risk/authorization classification. - Closure path: Sensitive call → PermissionsModule asks private reviewer → reviewer investigates transcript and read-only local evidence → returned verdict is consumed to allow/deny or grant just this call elevated capability; refusal/success enters main agent tool result and project audit log. - Boundary reachability: Auto is a supported installed session permission mode, and AutoModule supplies built-in reviewer to PermissionsModule; no user-written external reviewer skill is needed. - Claim being audited: Whether the main agent&#39;s particular proposed action is safe and authorized by user&#39;s actual request and current policy, not whether completed code is correct. - Ordinary reporting path: Main model&#39;s plan, proposed tool request and ordinary session context. - Complementary access path: Independent reviewer private agent with provenance-preserving transcript and read-only compute/history inspection against local evidence. - Independence boundary: Separate agent system/database, private identities and tools; reviewer cannot mutate the main agent&#39;s workspace; candidate action cannot approve itself. - Who acts on findings: PermissionsModule denies or permits the exact action (temporary elevation only for that call), then returns a refusal/execution result to main S1; project operator can inspect the recorded decision. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A distinct model actor makes a substantive risk and authorization judgment and the harness enforces it, rather than merely running deterministic ACL checks. - Evidence: Auto mode contract, private independent reviewer ownership, actual private reviewer send, returned allow/deny, one-call elevation, reviewer inspection surface. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: Scope is permission/safety auditing of a proposed operational action, not independent testing of produced code, whole-project S3 audit or a provider-independent second model. Requires configured functional Auto model route; a single reviewed action is the bounded claim.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No evidenced separate owner selecting an outside-future adaptation and returning it as a new capability to the whole installed project agent collective. - Disturbance / variety regulated: External tool APIs, market/task demands and prospective platform changes may require future capability redesign. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A distinct S4 decision would sense future external conditions, choose platform/project adaptation and enact a durable operating change. - Decision owner: Task agents can plan current user goals, and operators configure providers; no built-in prospective environmental decision owner is established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Persistent goals, model/provider switching, history/compaction, schedules and planned future module designs. - Closure path: Persisted goals and scheduled messages change task execution but do not close an independent future-organization adaptation judgment. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Task continuation, /goal, scheduling and model routing are current operational capabilities; roadmap master-plans are not shipped prospective decision loops. - Evidence: durable goal controls, native provider modes, scheduled module - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: Human-chosen permission modes and task goals constrain individual operations, but no explicit ultimate project identity/mandate issue with authoritative return is demonstrated. - Disturbance / variety regulated: The daemon may execute unsafe tools or need to reconcile differing user or organization demands. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A legitimate S5 owner would settle a concrete mission/identity or ultimate policy disagreement and return binding system-wide rules into later operations. - Decision owner: Operator selects session permissions and user goals; the reviewer judges each transient tool request, not ultimate organizational identity. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Workspace-write/Auto/read-only/full-access policy, session auth, persisted project config, temporary reviewer elevation. - Closure path: These settings constrain specific calls and sessions, but no independent organization policy issue, ultimate authority decision and returned project mandate is established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Permission selection, security review and account identity checks are not equivalent to S5&#39;s ultimate policy/identity reconciliation. - Evidence: session permission modes, per-call denial/elevation, task goal controls - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/7721d7e8967df09f3e07ece0fe10429fc2b958ab/assessments/ha/happy-agent.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 7721d7e8967df09f3e07ece0fe10429fc2b958ab\">7721d7e &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-22T02:26:06+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/wu1w/tevarn\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Tevarn</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-22\">2026-09-22</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/wu1w/tevarn/tree/e5e8e204ca2baf8ff671104ab6bf5529d40213ef\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision e5e8e204ca2baf8ff671104ab6bf5529d40213ef\">e5e8e20 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously execute owner-directed work through model-selected tool use and, when delegated, through persistent employee agent jobs. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous user tasks, repository/file state, shell/browser/web/data observations, tool errors, capability limits, model/context uncertainty and job-specific evidence requirements. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select substantive next actions/tools, interpret returned observations and decide what operational result is sufficient to return for the assigned work. - Decision owner: the running Tevarn model actor for the active session or persistent employee job. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: NexusAgentLoop, tool registry/executors, kernel process/token enforcement, permission/capability gates, context persistence, task grounding, completion gates and workforce inbox dispatch. - Closure path: owner/steward instruction → model decision → first-party tool execution → observation/evidence → subsequent model decision → final result persisted/returned; durable employee results return through workforce status/results to the steward organization. - Boundary reachability: the shipped NexusAgentLoop directly implements the model/tool feedback loop, and workforce jobs are dispatched into the same first-party runtime through the standard Identity + Inbox path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic runtime machinery constrains and executes actions, but the model chooses the substantive course of work and synthesizes the outcome rather than following a fixed workflow result. - Evidence: backend/agent/loop.py, backend/agent/workforcedispatch.py, backend/tools/builtins/agentopstools.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: external model providers host inference but do not inherit Tevarn organizational ownership; tool/runtime gates can terminate or constrain a run without becoming the S1 decision owner.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material first-party S2-specific coordination path is established at the reviewed organization boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the review looked for concrete oscillation, collision or interference among distinct Tevarn employee S1 units rather than treating delegation, inbox queues, dependency ordering or global concurrency limiting as coordination by name. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no material first-party autonomous or constructor decision path was established that specifically selects/revises a coordination response to an evidenced inter-S1 disturbance. - Decision owner: none established for qualifying S2. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: workforce inbox queues, cluster dependency ordering/semaphores and kernel run gating can order or limit execution, but the reviewed evidence does not tie them to a reconstructable S2-specific mutual-adjustment decision path among operational units. - Closure path: no qualifying inter-S1 disturbance → coordination judgment/response → changed later S1 behavior loop was established beyond generic queueing/scheduling/enforcement. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the steward decomposes and assigns work, but task allocation alone is not S2; the runtime can serialize or cap execution, but deterministic enforcement alone does not establish the required coordination discretion. - Evidence: backend/agent/workforcedispatch.py, backend/agent/clusterexecutor.py, backend/agent/loop.py run-gate path. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: Tevarn clearly supports multiple operational employees; this negative result is about the stricter S2 witness, not absence of multi-agent execution.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: regulate the current Tevarn organization by allocating work, budgets and capabilities, monitoring workforce state, intervening on failures/escalations and consolidating the current effort for the owner. - Disturbance / variety regulated: oversized/complex work, employee capability gaps, pending grants, budget exhaustion, failed/dead jobs, uneven work allocation, incomplete results and current organizational commitments that require intervention. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide whether to execute directly or use the workforce; split complex work; select employees; assign/reassign jobs; set or top up budgets; approve task-relevant capability escalation; react to failed/budget-exhausted work; gather results and determine the next current-control intervention. - Decision owner: the model-driven CEO/steward actor in the first-party steward contact mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: crewsteward list/hire/assign/status/results/pendinggrants/grantcaps/setbudget/budgets/topup operations, workforce inbox persistence/dispatcher, kernel capability tokens, deterministic token accounting and budget hard stops. - Closure path: current workforce/job/budget/grant state → steward model judgment → assignment/resource/capability/recovery action → persisted inbox/identity/kernel state changes → employees run under the changed state → status/results return for the next steward judgment. - Boundary reachability: workforcedispatch.py injects the steward organizational contract into supported steward contacts, and crewsteward exposes the corresponding first-party current-control actions directly in the shipped runtime. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the steward contract assigns current organizational choices to the model actor; budget/capability/run machinery enforces the selected constraints but does not decide which worker, budget change, grant or recovery action should be used. - Evidence: backend/agent/workforcedispatch.py, backend/tools/builtins/crewstewardtools.py, backend/agent/workforcebudget.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: simple task delegation alone is not used for this mapping; the positive state depends on the broader whole-workforce status/resource/escalation loop and enforceable intervention rights. - Whole-system current view: the steward can inspect the active workforce, per-employee identity/capabilities, queued/running job status/results, pending grants and budget/usage state across the organization it is managing. - Current-control decision scope: current work allocation, employee participation, per-job/persistent token budgets, capability escalation, requeue/recovery decisions and consolidation of current organizational outcomes.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no sufficiently independent complementary audit path is established for the assessed Tevarn organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incorrect or weak operational claims were inspected through grounding and cluster-review mechanisms, but the available paths do not establish complementary access to operational reality independent enough to qualify as S3. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no qualifying independent audit owner/path was established that can inspect materially different evidence from the ordinary production/reporting path and feed findings back into control. - Decision owner: none established for qualifying S3. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: taskgrounding.py requires evidence/tool usage for hallucination-prone task classes; cluster review can parse returned deliverables and ask a reviewer model for verdicts; completion gates can require further work. These are useful verification controls but do not by themselves supply independent operational access. - Closure path: grounding/review can nudge, revise or reject a response, but the reviewed evidence did not establish an independent audit channel with materially different access to the underlying operational reality. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model-based reviewer judgment may be agentic, but reviewer naming or a second model is insufficient when its access remains the produced deliverable/ordinary evidence path rather than an independent complementary audit surface. - Evidence: backend/agent/taskgrounding.py, backend/agent/clusterexecutor.py, completion-gate surfaces in backend/agent/. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a future mode that gives a distinct reviewer direct raw-artifact/replay/probe access could change this classification without changing S1/S3.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party outside-and-then adaptation loop is established at the reviewed boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing external conditions, future opportunities/threats and learned owner preferences were inspected, but no shipped path closes sensing → prospective option generation → adaptation of present organizational capability. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no autonomous or constructor owner/path was established that turns external/future distinctions into adaptation options and returns a chosen option into current S3/capability. - Decision owner: none established for qualifying S4. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: web/browser tools can sense the environment; memory services can persist information; autoremember.py heuristically stores selected chat decisions/history; skills/MCP/configuration can change capability when explicitly operated. - Closure path: no qualifying environmental/future distinction → generated adaptation option → returned capability/current-control change loop is supplied by the standard distribution. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: autoremember.py explicitly performs no LLM call and uses regex/length rules to store notes; memory persistence or available web search therefore does not establish prospective agent-owned adaptation. - Evidence: backend/agent/autoremember.py, reviewed memory/tool/skill surfaces and steward tooling. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: an ordinary task may research future facts, but task-local research is not credited as organization-level S4 unless it develops and closes a prospective adaptation option into present capability.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Function: preserve ultimate purpose/direction by reserving strategic ambiguity and project-level direction decisions to the legitimate owner above the steward organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: ambiguity over strategic direction, owner intent, project-level commitments or decisions whose consequences should not be settled by the steward as routine current control. - Decisive decision or feedback right: answer strategy-level clarification and set/approve the direction or project decision that the steward must then operationalize. - Decision owner: the human owner as legitimate parent authority for the assessed personal-agent organization. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: the steward role contract explicitly separates owner vs CEO responsibilities; first-party clarify can block and return an owner answer; ordinary session state then carries the returned instruction into subsequent model/tool/workforce decisions. - Closure path: strategic/identity-level ambiguity encountered by steward → clarify/owner conversation reaches the owner → owner decides direction/project point → answer returns into the active session → steward decomposes/assigns/acts under that returned direction. - Boundary reachability: owner contact is part of the ordinary supported personal-Agent-OS boundary, the steward contract explicitly names the owner as direction/project decision authority, and the shipped clarify tool provides the runtime return channel. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: no first-party internal ultimate-authority mode was established; the CEO/steward is explicitly subordinate on direction/strategic clarification while retaining current S3 authority, so the qualifying S5 closure is parent-governed rather than autonomous. - Evidence: backend/agent/workforcedispatch.py, backend/tools/builtins/agentopstools.py (clarify). - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: ordinary permission confirmation, task approval or capability escalation is not counted as S5; the positive mapping is limited to the explicit owner-reserved direction/strategy path. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: the organization&#39;s owner-directed purpose/strategic direction and project-level choices that define what the steward organization should pursue rather than how to execute current work. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: parent mode only — the human owner; no first-party autonomous or constructor S5 mode is established at this boundary. - Return-to-operation path: the owner answer returns through the active owner/steward session, after which the steward&#39;s model/tool/workforce decisions are made under the clarified direction.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/69ab172a63ce5eacb442e6d85020ca9232eae676/assessments/tevarn.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 69ab172a63ce5eacb442e6d85020ca9232eae676\">69ab172 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-12T21:05:50+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/karlorz/atomcode\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AtomCode</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-12\">2026-10-12</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/karlorz/atomcode/tree/152b2cc132dda9b177c3a030e21cfacd95494ea3\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 152b2cc132dda9b177c3a030e21cfacd95494ea3\">152b2cc &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform and verify software changes through the model/tool/feedback loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: user requests, repository contents, compiler/test failures, tool results, budget/goal conditions, provider output and permissions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next coding tool/action, revise a patch based on feedback, invoke review, and decide task completion. - Decision owner: main model-backed coding agent (and bounded child coding models for delegated file tasks). - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: kernel Agent and TurnRunner, coding tool registry, permissions, turn budgets, session, goal evaluator, post-edit verification, notifications. - Closure path: prompt → model-chosen code/tool action → first-party tool execution → returned observation → further model reasoning/action or final response. - Boundary reachability: the standard coding-agent assembly builds this loop and registers first-party tools for interactive/headless use. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the runtime enforces scope, timeouts and dispatch, but the model retains the task-specific code operation choices. - Evidence: kernel agent, coding assembly, core loop. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: external model/provider availability is a dependency rather than a credited system owner.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no bounded first-party inter-S1 interference-specific coordination/feedback loop is evidenced. - Disturbance / variety regulated: parallel child edits could conflict over a shared repository, but deterministic per-file task partition, queueing and concurrency caps do not show incompatible-change detection/attenuation with returned child correction. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no distinct S2-specific arbitration right established. - Decision owner: not established for S2. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SubAgentPool concurrent JoinSet, per-file SubAgentTask, timeout/retry limits, child tool filtering, progress/result events. - Closure path: task results return to the main coding model, but no specific inter-S1 conflicting-operation attenuation/return relationship was reconstructed. - Boundary reachability: no positive S2 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: selecting one file per child reduces a possible overlap by design, but this is decomposition/serialization; it is not a demonstrated organizational S2 controller that senses interaction disturbances and changes subsequent S1 work. - Evidence: paralleledit.rs, background.rs. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: does not mean agents can never be assembled into an S2-capable multi-team configuration; no unsupported cross-agent owner imported here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no distinct current whole-system manager owning resource/commitment discretion across a live set of S1 units was reconstructed from the shipped modes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: goal progress, turn caps and individual background sessions are tracked, but this is operation-level control. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no separate whole-current portfolio/priority/commitment decision over multiple S1 units. - Decision owner: not established for S3. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: goal-evaluation continuation, turn-level progress/budget guard, per-file subagent pool, background task session control, CLI/TUI status. - Closure path: current coding work may continue or stop based on model feedback and deterministic budgets, without a separate S3 current supervisory return. - Boundary reachability: no positive S3 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: goal-mode agents and deterministic background schedulers act on bounded coding tasks; they do not automatically own overall current organizational commitments. - Evidence: goal evaluator, goal lifecycle, subagent pool, background agent. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: the model has real choice within individual tasks; that is credited S1, not general whole-current management.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: in the separately callable shipped codereview mode, independently inspect actual changed source and Git diff, judge implementation defects, and return structured findings for coding-agent correction. - Disturbance / variety regulated: divergence between an implementer&#39;s reported success and defects in actual changed code, APIs, correctness/security/reliability implications. - Decisive decision or feedback right: independently decide/report defect findings from a direct evidence path, not merely relay implementing agent&#39;s own tool outcomes. - Decision owner: distinct read-only reviewer Agent with a dedicated persona, message context and tool set; the implementing main model owns follow-up corrective code operations. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: first-party ReviewTool, live git diff, diff line annotations and impact/risk plan, review-only registry, path confinement, structured reportfinding sink, change-scope filter and findings sorting, provider reuse. - Closure path: parent/host model invokes codereview → tool obtains Git diff from LIVE workspace → separate reviewer inspects surrounding source, reports findings → tool returns structured evidence to host model → host can revise code and re-run checks/review. - Boundary reachability: shipped PrepareOptions::default().review=true, parts.rs registers ReviewTool in coding agent tool registry, fills its shared provider on assembly; the codereview tool then instantiates a fresh restricted read-only reviewer with direct repository access. No custom extension or benchmark-only setup is needed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the separate reviewer model determines actual defect judgments, while Git diff retrieval, access confinement and rendering are deterministic supports; a common provider does not share agent history or mutable authority. - Claim being audited: actual worktree/staged/base code changes relative to the implementation task, not only the main agent&#39;s self-report. - Ordinary reporting path: main coding-agent messages, tool results, tests and same-agent post-edit verification. - Complementary access path: independent reviewer starts from live Git diff, directly reads/greps/inspects source via its restricted tool registry, and writes structured reportfinding judgments. - Independence boundary: fresh read-only reviewer agent with own persona/messages and no write/edit/bash tools, path-confined to repo; possible shared LLM provider/model family is a dependency and not a common decision context. - Who acts on findings: invoking primary coding model receives prioritized results and may modify code or request more tests; review tool itself remains read-only. - Evidence: ReviewTool, read-only child build, coding mount, path guard. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: available as a separately triggered tool; not necessarily executed on every coding turn. The source confirms independence of reviewer context/tool authority, not that every reviewer verdict is correct. Ordinary same-agent verification by verify.rs is expressly excluded as the positive audit witness.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no first-party prospective external environment-intelligence loop culminating in adaptation of the organization&#39;s future capability was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: current requests, provider/tool outcomes and stored past session information are available, but no distinct outside-and-then intelligence function is shown. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no future organizational capability adaptation owner proven. - Decision owner: none established for S4. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: persistent sessions, memory/context, web tools, code graphs, goal mode, skills, provider/model selection. - Closure path: current task information may inform subsequent tasks, but an external future signal → adaptation option → model/parent-owned capability change → returned altered operation was not established. - Boundary reachability: no positive S4 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: agentic browsing and persistent context can support immediate coding operations; their existence does not establish strategic external intelligence ownership. - Evidence: coding assembly, goal evaluator, README feature boundary. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: possible user-defined future extensions not bundled/wired in the reviewed mode cannot be credited.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no constitutional identity/purpose reconciliation loop with an ultimate first-party decision owner and authoritative return was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: tool risk, task objectives and provider configuration are enforcement and operational constraints, not a whole-system identity or ultimate purpose matter. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none for organization-level ultimate policy/identity in the declared boundary. - Decision owner: none established for S5; user authorization does not automatically create an S5 parent mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: system prompt, tool permission policies, sensitive path checks, approval screens, provider settings, remote access/session controls. - Closure path: a user may allow or deny an edit or switch a model, but there is no declared identity-policy matter → legitimate ultimate authority → binding returned policy/identity decision loop. - Boundary reachability: no positive S5 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model leaves fixed permissions, not a model-owned ultimate organizational charter. - Evidence: coding assembly, README safety/modes. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: upstream human product/OSS governance is an adjacent actor unless a supported runtime loop binds it as an S5 parent decision right.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/80fc101edd018461ab3b14a5b707af6977cda275/assessments/atomcode.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 80fc101edd018461ab3b14a5b707af6977cda275\">80fc101 &#8599;</a></td></tr>"];
